# The names of MATLAB. See names.rs for the form of a line.

# operators by their names
times||2|2|a .* b: the product entry by entry
rdivide||2|2|a ./ b: the quotient entry by entry
ldivide||2|2|a .\ b: b divided by a entry by entry
power||2|2|a .^ b: the power entry by entry
plus||2|*|a + b
minus||2|2|a - b
uminus||1|1|-a
uplus||1|1|+a
mtimes||2|*|a*b: the product of matrices
mpower||2|2|A^k: the power of a matrix
mldivide||2|2|A \ b: the solution x of A*x = b (of least squares when A is not square)
mrdivide||2|2|b / A: the solution x of x*A = b
colon||2|3|a:b and a:step:b: the numbers from a to b

# arrays
linspace||2|3|linspace(a, b, n): n numbers from a to b in equal steps (100 without n)
logspace||2|3|logspace(a, b, n): n powers of ten from 10^a to 10^b, the exponents in equal steps (50 without n)
meshgrid||1|2|meshgrid(x, y): the matrices [X, Y] of a grid: the rows of X are x, the columns of Y are y
ndgrid||1|2|ndgrid(x, y): the matrices [X, Y] of a grid: the columns of X are x, the rows of Y are y
repmat||2|3|repmat(A, m, n): m by n copies of A; repmat(A, n): n by n
repelem||2|3|repelem(list, n): every entry n times; repelem(A, r, c): every entry as r by c entries
horzcat||1|*|arrays side by side
vertcat||1|*|arrays one above the other
cat||2|*|cat(1, A, B): one above the other; cat(2, A, B): side by side
blkdiag||1|*|the matrix with the given blocks along its diagonal
flip||1|2|a list in the opposite order; flip(A): the rows; flip(A, 2): the columns
fliplr||1|1|the columns in the opposite order
flipud||1|1|the rows in the opposite order
rot90||1|2|rot90(A, k): k quarter turns against the clock (one without k)
circshift||2|3|circshift(list, k): moved k places round; circshift(A, k): the rows; circshift(A, k, 2): the columns; circshift(A, [down, right])
tril||1|2|tril(A, k): the entries on and below diagonal k (the main one without k), zeros elsewhere
triu||1|2|triu(A, k): the entries on and above diagonal k, zeros elsewhere
sortrows||1|4|the rows in rising order; sortrows(A, k): by column k; sortrows(A, -k): falling; sortrows(A, [k, m]); sortrows(table[, [names]][, "ascend" | "descend"]): the rows of a table by its variables
issorted||1|2|whether a list rises; issorted(x, "descend"), issorted(x, "either"); a matrix: every column
setxor||2|2|the entries that are in one of two lists only, in rising order
ismember||2|2|ismember(x, list): whether x is an entry; for a list x one answer for each entry
isequal||2|*|whether the arguments are equal as values
numel||1|1|the number of entries of an array
size||1|2|the sizes of an array as MATLAB counts them: [1, n] for a flat list; size(A, k): size k
ndims||1|1|the number of sizes of an array: 2 for numbers, lists and matrices
isempty||1|1|whether a list or a text is empty
isrow||1|1|whether an array is one row
iscolumn||1|1|whether an array is one column
isvector||1|1|whether an array is one row or one column
isscalar||1|1|whether an array is one entry
ismatrix||1|1|whether an array has two sizes
isdiag||1|1|whether a matrix is diagonal
istril||1|1|whether a matrix is lower triangular
istriu||1|1|whether a matrix is upper triangular
bandwidth||1|2|[lower, upper]: how far below and above the diagonal the entries of a matrix reach; bandwidth(A, "lower") or "upper": one of them; bandwidth(system[, drop]): the first frequency where the gain has fallen 3 dB (or by the drop, as -6) below its value at frequency 0
isbanded||3|3|isbanded(A, lower, upper): whether A reaches no farther from its diagonal
issymmetric||1|2|whether A = transpose(A); issymmetric(A, "skew"): A = -transpose(A)
ishermitian||1|2|whether A is its own conjugate transpose; ishermitian(A, "skew")
nnz||1|1|the number of entries that are not zero, or of conditions that hold: nnz([1, 5, 9] > 2)
nonzeros||1|1|the entries that are not zero, column after column
find||1|2|the places of the entries that are not zero, or where a condition holds: find(x > 2); [row, column] in a matrix; find(x, n): the first n
any||1|2|whether an entry is not zero or a condition holds; a matrix column by column; any(A, 2): row by row; any(A, "all")
all||1|2|whether no entry is zero or a condition holds everywhere; a matrix column by column; all(A, 2); all(A, "all")
ind2sub||2|2|ind2sub([rows, columns], k): [row, column] of entry k counted down the columns
sub2ind||3|3|sub2ind([rows, columns], row, column): the number of the entry counted down the columns
magic||1|1|the magic square of MATLAB: all rows, all columns and both diagonals have one sum
invhilb||1|1|the inverse of the Hilbert matrix, exactly
pascal||1|2|the matrix of Pascal's triangle; pascal(n, 1): its lower triangular root; pascal(n, 2): a cube root of the identity
rosser||0|0|Rosser's matrix: 8 by 8, with eigenvalues that are hard to tell apart
wilkinson||1|1|Wilkinson's matrix: tridiagonal, with pairs of nearly equal eigenvalues
toeplitz||1|2|toeplitz(c, r): constant along every diagonal, first column c, first row r; toeplitz(r): symmetric
hankel||1|2|hankel(c): constant along every antidiagonal, first column c, zeros below; hankel(c, r): last row r
compan||1|1|the companion matrix of the polynomial with the given coefficients (the highest first): its eigenvalues are the roots
hadamard||1|1|a matrix of 1 and -1 with orthogonal rows; the size is a power of two, or 12 or 20 times one
vander||1|1|the Vandermonde matrix of MATLAB: the powers fall from left to right
ctranspose||1|1|the conjugate transpose
nchoosek||2|2|nchoosek(n, k): the binomial coefficient; nchoosek(list, k): all choices of k entries
perms||1|1|all orders of a list, the last one first, as MATLAB lists them

# sums and statistics of data
!total|1|2|the sum of the entries; also sum(list); sum(A): of every column; sum(A, 2): of every row; sum(A, "all")
!accumulate|1|2|the running sums of a list; of a matrix down its columns; cumsum(A, 2): along its rows
!median|1|2|
!mode|1|2|
!variance|1|3|sample variance: squared distances from the mean, summed, over n - 1; variance of a distribution; var(x, 1): over n
!stddev|1|3|sample standard deviation, the root of the variance; of a distribution too; std(x, 1): of the population
+variance|var
+stddev|std
+geometricmean|geomean
+harmonicmean|harmmean
cumprod||1|2|the running products of a list; of a matrix down its columns; cumprod(A, 2): along its rows
cummax||1|2|the largest entry so far
cummin||1|2|the smallest entry so far
bounds||1|2|[smallest, largest]
peak2peak||1|2|the largest entry less the smallest
maxk||2|2|maxk(list, k): the k largest entries
mink||2|2|mink(list, k): the k smallest entries
movmean||2|3|movmean(x, k): the mean of the k entries about every entry (fewer at the ends); movmean(x, [back, ahead])
movsum||2|3|the sum over a moving window, as movmean
movmedian||2|3|the median over a moving window, as movmean
movmax||2|3|the largest entry of a moving window, as movmean
movmin||2|3|the smallest entry of a moving window, as movmean
movstd||2|3|the standard deviation over a moving window, as movmean
movvar||2|3|the variance over a moving window, as movmean
movprod||2|3|the product over a moving window, as movmean
movmad||2|3|the median distance from the median over a moving window, as movmean
trapz||1|2|trapz(y): the area under the points by the rule of trapezes, one unit apart; trapz(x, y): at the places x
cumtrapz||1|2|the running areas of trapz
prctile|percentile|2|3|prctile(x, p): the value at p percent of the data as MATLAB takes it; p may be a list
zscore||1|3|every entry less the mean, over the standard deviation; zscore(x, 1): the one of the population
rescale||1|3|the entries moved and scaled to run from 0 to 1; rescale(x, a, b): from a to b
trimmean||2|3|trimmean(x, p): the mean without the p percent of the entries that lie farthest out, half at each end
mad||1|3|the mean distance of the entries from their mean; mad(x, 1): the median distance from the median
cov||1|3|cov(x): the variance; cov(x, y): the matrix of the variances and the covariance; cov(A): of the columns of A
corrcoef||1|3|corrcoef(x, y): the matrix [[1, r], [r, 1]] of the correlation; corrcoef(A): of the columns of A
histcounts||1|2|histcounts(x, edges): how many entries fall between every two edges; histcounts(x, n): in n bins of one width; histcounts(c): how many values made by categorical are of every kind
detrend||1|2|the data less its straight line of least squares; detrend(x, n): less the polynomial of degree n
unwrap||1|2|angles with the jumps of pi or more taken out by adding whole turns
accumarray||2|3|accumarray(places, values): entry k is the sum of the values whose place is k
tabulate||1|1|the rows [value, how often, percent] of the distinct entries
tiedrank||1|1|the ranks of the entries, equal entries sharing the mean of their ranks

# matrices taken apart
!norm|1|2|absolute value of a number, Euclidean length of a vector; norm(v, p): the p-norm, p = inf the largest entry; of a matrix the largest singular value; norm(A, 1), norm(A, inf), norm(A, "fro"); norm(system) as h2norm, norm(system, inf) as hinfnorm
!eigenvalues|1|2|eigenvalues of a matrix; eig(A, B): the numbers x with det(A - x*B) = 0
+eigenvalues|eig
+singularvalues|svd
+nullspace|null
+adjugate|adjoint
+hilbert|hilb
cond||1|2|the condition number of a matrix: the largest singular value over the smallest; cond(A, 1), cond(A, inf), cond(A, "fro")
rcond||1|1|one over the condition number in the 1-norm
vecnorm||1|3|the lengths of the columns of a matrix; vecnorm(A, p): their p-norms; vecnorm(A, p, 2): of the rows
orth||1|1|a matrix whose columns are orthonormal and span the columns of A
colspace||1|1|a matrix whose columns are a basis of the span of the columns of A
lu||1|1|[L, U, P] with P*A = L*U: L lower triangular with ones, U upper triangular, P swaps rows
chol||1|2|the upper triangular R with R'*R = A for a positive definite A; chol(A, "lower"): L with L*L' = A
jordan||1|1|the Jordan form of a matrix
jordandecomposition||1|1|[S, J] with A*S = S*J and J the Jordan form
diagonalize||1|1|[V, D] with A*V = V*D: the columns of V are eigenvectors, D is diagonal
smithform||1|1|the Smith form of a matrix of whole numbers: diagonal, every entry dividing the next
smithdecomposition||1|1|[U, V, S] with U*A*V = S the Smith form, U and V of determinant 1 or -1
hermiteform||1|1|the Hermite form of a matrix of whole numbers: upper triangular, reached by whole steps with rows
hermitedecomposition||1|1|[U, H] with U*A = H the Hermite form, U of determinant 1 or -1
schur||1|1|the upper triangular T of the Schur form A = Q*T*Q', in decimals
schurdecomposition||1|1|[Q, T] with A = Q*T*Q', Q unitary and T upper triangular, in decimals
hess||1|1|the Hessenberg form of a matrix: zero below the first subdiagonal, in decimals
hessenbergdecomposition||1|1|[P, H] with A = P*H*P', P unitary, in decimals
singularvaluedecomposition||1|1|[U, S, V] with A = U*S*V': U and V unitary, S diagonal with the singular values, in decimals
sylvester||3|3|sylvester(A, B, C): the matrix X with A*X + X*B = C
subspace||2|2|the angle between the spans of the columns of two matrices
planerot||1|1|planerot([x, y]): [G, [r, 0]] with the turn G that takes [x, y] to [r, 0]

# polynomials as lists of coefficients, the highest first
!roots|1|2|roots of a polynomial; roots([1, -3, 2]): of the one with these coefficients, the highest first
!residue|2|3|residue(f, z, a): the residue of f at z = a; residue(b, a) with lists of coefficients: [residues, poles, polynomial part] of b(s)/a(s); residue(r, p, k): back to [b, a]
+degree|polynomialdegree
poly||1|1|poly([roots]): the coefficients of the polynomial with these roots; poly(A): of the characteristic polynomial of a matrix
polyval||2|2|polyval(p, x): the value at x of the polynomial with the coefficients p; x may be a list
polyvalm||2|2|polyvalm(p, A): the polynomial with the coefficients p of the matrix A
polyfit||3|3|polyfit(x, y, n): the coefficients of the polynomial of degree n that fits the points best (least squares)
polyder||1|2|the coefficients of the derivative; polyder(a, b): of the derivative of the product
polyint||1|2|the coefficients of the integral; polyint(p, c): with the constant c
conv||2|3|conv(a, b): the coefficients of the product of two polynomials, the convolution of two lists; conv(a, b, "same"), conv(a, b, "valid")
deconv||2|2|deconv(u, v): [quotient, remainder] of the division of polynomials given by their coefficients
poly2sym||1|2|poly2sym(c, x): the polynomial in x with the coefficients c
sym2poly||1|2|sym2poly(p, x): all coefficients of a polynomial, the highest first
coeffs||1|3|coeffs(p, x): the coefficients that are not zero, the lowest first; coeffs(p, x, "all"): all, the highest first
horner||1|2|a polynomial in nested form: ((a*x + b)*x + c)*x + d
numden||1|1|[numerator, denominator] without fractions in either
quorem||2|3|quorem(a, b): [quotient, remainder] of whole numbers or, with a variable, of polynomials
interp1||2|5|interp1(x, y, q): the value at q of the straight lines through the points; interp1(x, y, q, method): "nearest", "next", "previous", "pchip", "spline", "makima"; "extrap" after it goes on outside the points; q may be a list or a name
spline||3|3|spline(x, y, q): the value at q of the cubic spline through the points (smooth to the second derivative)
pchip||3|3|pchip(x, y, q): the value at q of the cubic pieces through the points that keep the shape of the data
makima||3|3|makima(x, y, q): the value at q of Akima's cubic pieces through the points, without overshoot
interp2||5|6|interp2(x, y, Z, xq, yq): the value between the points of a grid, straight in both directions; "nearest" as a sixth argument
gbasis|groebnerbasis|1|2|gbasis([polynomials], [variables]): a reduced Groebner basis in the lexicographic order of the variables

# signals
!select|2|4|select(list, f): the entries for which f is true; filter(b, a, x) with three lists: the filter of lfilter; filter(b, a, x, zi): from the state zi
fft||1|2|the discrete Fourier transform of a list: entry k is the sum of x[j]*e^(-2 pi i j k/n); fft(x, n): of n points; of a matrix column by column
ifft||1|2|the inverse of fft
fft2||1|1|the Fourier transform of a matrix in both directions
ifft2||1|1|the inverse of fft2
fftshift||1|1|the halves of a list swapped, so that the middle of a transform comes first
ifftshift||1|1|the inverse of fftshift
dct||1|2|the discrete cosine transform (the second kind, with orthonormal waves)
idct||1|2|the inverse of dct
lfilter||2|4|lfilter(b, a, x): the output y of the filter a[1]*y[n] = b[1]*x[n] + b[2]*x[n-1] + ... - a[2]*y[n-1] - ...; lfilter(butter(2, 0.3), x): b and a as the pair that a function that makes filters gives; lfilter(b, a, x, zi): from the state zi that filtic makes of earlier outputs and inputs
conv2||2|3|conv2(A, B): the convolution of two matrices; conv2(A, B, "same"), conv2(A, B, "valid")
xcorr||1|2|xcorr(x, y): the correlation of two lists at every shift, 2n - 1 values; xcorr(x): of x with itself
upsample||2|2|upsample(x, n): n - 1 zeros after every entry
downsample||2|3|downsample(x, n): every n-th entry; downsample(x, n, k): from entry k + 1 on

# functions of numbers by the names of MATLAB
!round|1|3|nearest whole number, halves away from zero, of each part; round(x, n): to n digits after the point; round(x, n, "significant"): to n digits
!rem|2|3|polynomial remainder; rem(a, b) of numbers: a - b*fix(a/b), with the sign of a
!expint|1|2|expint(n, z): exponential integral E_n(z), the integral of e^(-z t)/t^n from 1 on; expint(z) = E_1(z)
!digamma|1|2|digamma function; psi(k, z): its k-th derivative, the polygamma function
!kroneckerdelta|1|*|1 when the arguments are equal, else 0; kroneckerdelta(m): 1 when m = 0
!angle|1|3|angle between two vectors, lines or planes; angle(a, b, c): at the vertex b; angle(z): the argument of a complex number
+trunc|fix
+lgamma|gammaln
+si|sinint
+ci|cosint
+shi|sinhint
+chi|coshint
+li|logint
+hypergeometric|hypergeom
+uppergamma|igamma
+jacobi|jacobisymbol
+powmod|powermod
+eulernumber|euler
expm1||1|1|e^x - 1
log1p||1|1|ln(1 + x)
nthroot||2|2|nthroot(x, n): the real n-th root of a real number: nthroot(-8, 3) = -2
realsqrt||1|1|the square root, an error when it is not real
reallog||1|1|the natural logarithm, an error when it is not real
realpow||2|2|realpow(x, y): x^y, an error when it is not real
nextpow2||1|1|the least whole p with 2^p >= abs(x)
pow2||1|2|pow2(x) = 2^x; pow2(f, e) = f*2^e
idivide||2|3|idivide(a, b): a/b rounded toward zero; idivide(a, b, "round" | "floor" | "ceil")
sind||1|1|sine of an angle in degrees
cosd||1|1|cosine of an angle in degrees
tand||1|1|tangent of an angle in degrees
cotd||1|1|cotangent of an angle in degrees
secd||1|1|secant of an angle in degrees
cscd||1|1|cosecant of an angle in degrees
asind||1|1|inverse sine in degrees
acosd||1|1|inverse cosine in degrees
atand||1|1|inverse tangent in degrees
acotd||1|1|inverse cotangent in degrees
asecd||1|1|inverse secant in degrees
acscd||1|1|inverse cosecant in degrees
atan2d||2|2|atan2d(y, x): the angle of the point (x, y) in degrees
sinpi||1|1|sin(pi*x)
cospi||1|1|cos(pi*x)
deg2rad||1|1|degrees as radians
rad2deg||1|1|radians as degrees
cart2pol||2|3|cart2pol(x, y): [angle, radius]; cart2pol(x, y, z): [angle, radius, z]
pol2cart||2|3|pol2cart(angle, radius): [x, y]; pol2cart(angle, radius, z): [x, y, z]
cart2sph||3|3|cart2sph(x, y, z): [azimuth, elevation, radius]
sph2cart||3|3|sph2cart(azimuth, elevation, radius): [x, y, z]
rat||1|2|rat(x): a fraction within a millionth of x, by continued fractions; rat(x, tol): within tol
bitand||2|2|the bits that both whole numbers have
bitor||2|2|the bits that either whole number has
bitxor||2|2|the bits that one of two whole numbers has
bitshift||2|3|bitshift(a, k): a*2^k, bits lost on the right when k is negative; bitshift(a, k, n): the low n bits of it
bitcmp||1|2|bitcmp(a): every bit of the 64 turned; bitcmp(a, n) or bitcmp(a, "uint8"): of that many bits
bitget||2|2|bitget(a, k): bit k of a, counted from 1 at the lowest
bitset||2|3|bitset(a, k): a with bit k set; bitset(a, k, 0): cleared
erfcx||1|1|the scaled complementary error function e^(x^2)*erfc(x)
dawson||1|1|Dawson's integral e^(-x^2) times the integral of e^(t^2) from 0 to x
betaln||2|2|the logarithm of the beta function
ssinint||1|1|the shifted sine integral Si(x) - pi/2
airy||1|2|airy(x) = Ai(x); airy(k, x): Ai for k = 0, Ai' for 1, Bi for 2, Bi' for 3
besselh||2|3|besselh(nu, x): the Hankel function of the first kind; besselh(nu, k, x): of kind k = 1 or 2
whittakerm||3|3|whittakerm(a, b, z): Whittaker's function M
whittakerw||3|3|whittakerw(a, b, z): Whittaker's function W
wrightomega||1|1|Wright's omega function: the solution w of w + ln(w) = x
rectangularpulse||1|3|rectangularpulse(a, b, x): 1 between a and b, 1/2 at the ends, 0 outside; rectangularpulse(x): between -1/2 and 1/2
triangularpulse||1|4|triangularpulse(a, b, c, x): rises from 0 at a to 1 at b and falls to 0 at c; triangularpulse(a, c, x): b in the middle; triangularpulse(x): -1, 0, 1
jacobicd||2|2|the Jacobi elliptic function cd = cn/dn
jacobisd||2|2|the Jacobi elliptic function sd = sn/dn
jacobind||2|2|the Jacobi elliptic function nd = 1/dn
jacobidc||2|2|the Jacobi elliptic function dc = dn/cn
jacobinc||2|2|the Jacobi elliptic function nc = 1/cn
jacobisc||2|2|the Jacobi elliptic function sc = sn/cn
jacobins||2|2|the Jacobi elliptic function ns = 1/sn
jacobids||2|2|the Jacobi elliptic function ds = dn/sn
jacobics||2|2|the Jacobi elliptic function cs = cn/sn
jacobiam||2|2|jacobiam(u, m): the amplitude of Jacobi's elliptic functions, for real u and m from 0 to under 1
jacobizeta||2|2|jacobizeta(u, m): Jacobi's zeta function, for real u and m from 0 to under 1
ellipticck||1|1|the complementary complete elliptic integral of the first kind K(1 - m)
ellipticce||1|1|the complementary complete elliptic integral of the second kind E(1 - m)
ellipticcpi||2|2|the complementary complete elliptic integral of the third kind Pi(n, 1 - m)
ellipke||1|1|ellipke(m): [K(m), E(m)], the complete elliptic integrals of the first and second kind
ellipj||2|2|ellipj(u, m): [sn, cn, dn], Jacobi's elliptic functions
gammaincinv||2|2|gammaincinv(p, a): the x with gammap(a, x) = p
betaincinv||3|3|betaincinv(p, a, b): the x with betaregularized(x, a, b) = p

# texts
!length|1|1|number of entries of a list, of characters of a text
!reverse|1|1|a list or a text in the opposite order
!join|1|*|the entries of several lists as one list; several texts as one
!contains|2|2|contains(list, x): whether x is an entry; contains(text, part): whether the part stands in the text
!count|2|2|count(list, x): how often x is an entry; count(text, part): how often the part stands in the text
num2str||1|2|a number as a text, with about five digits; num2str(x, n): with n digits
int2str||1|1|a number rounded to a whole one, as a text
mat2str||1|2|a matrix as the text that MATLAB reads: [1 2;3 4]; mat2str(A, n): with n digits
tostring|string|1|1|a value as the text that prints it
dec2bin||1|2|a whole number as a text of binary digits; dec2bin(n, k): of k digits or more
dec2hex||1|2|a whole number as a text of hexadecimal digits; dec2hex(n, k): of k digits or more
dec2base||2|3|dec2base(n, b): a whole number as a text of digits of base b up to 36; dec2base(n, b, k): of k digits or more
bin2dec||1|1|the number that a text of binary digits writes
hex2dec||1|1|the number that a text of hexadecimal digits writes
base2dec||2|2|base2dec(text, b): the number that a text of digits of base b writes
char||1|*|the text of the characters with the given numbers; several texts as one
double||1|1|the numbers of the characters of a text; of a value its number in decimals
strcat||1|*|texts and numbers as one text
strlength|strlen|1|1|the number of characters of a text
upper|toupper|1|1|a text in capital letters
lower|tolower|1|1|a text in small letters
strtrim|strip|1|1|a text without the blanks at its ends
strrep||3|3|strrep(text, old, new): the text with every old part replaced by the new one
strfind||2|2|strfind(text, part): the places where the part stands in the text
strsplit||1|2|the parts of a text between blanks; strsplit(text, between): between that text
strjoin||1|2|strjoin([texts], between): the texts as one, with blanks or the given text between them
strcmp||2|2|whether two texts are the same
strcmpi||2|2|whether two texts are the same but for capitals
strncmp||3|3|strncmp(a, b, n): whether the first n characters of two texts are the same
strncmpi||3|3|strncmpi(a, b, n): as strncmp, but for capitals
startswith||2|2|whether a text starts with another
endswith||2|2|whether a text ends with another
blanks||1|1|a text of n blanks
isletter||1|1|for every character of a text whether it is a letter
isspace||1|1|for every character of a text whether it is a blank
ischar|isstring|1|1|whether a value is a text
isnumeric||1|1|whether a value is a number or an array of numbers
islogical||1|1|whether a value is true or false, or an array of such
logical||1|1|true for numbers other than zero and for conditions that hold, entry by entry
sprintf||1|*|sprintf(format, values): a text with the values in the forms %d %i %u %f %e %g %s %c %x %o, as in C; \n is a new line
rats||1|2|a fraction near a number, as a text

# the distribution functions of MATLAB
!inv|1|3|reciprocal, inverse of a matrix; inv(system): the system the other way round; finv(p, v1, v2): the place where the F distribution reaches the probability p
+quantile|icdf
normpdf||1|3|normpdf(x, mu, sigma): density or probability at x of the normal distribution with the mean mu (0) and the deviation sigma (1)
normcdf||1|4|normcdf(x, mu, sigma): probability of a value up to x of the normal distribution with the mean mu (0) and the deviation sigma (1); "upper" as the last argument: of a value above x
norminv||1|3|norminv(p, mu, sigma): the place where the probability p is reached of the normal distribution with the mean mu (0) and the deviation sigma (1)
normrnd||0|2|normrnd(mu, sigma): a random number of the normal distribution with the mean mu (0) and the deviation sigma (1)
normstat||0|2|normstat(mu, sigma): [mean, variance] of the normal distribution with the mean mu (0) and the deviation sigma (1)
unifpdf||1|3|unifpdf(x, a, b): density or probability at x of the uniform distribution from a (0) to b (1)
unifcdf||1|4|unifcdf(x, a, b): probability of a value up to x of the uniform distribution from a (0) to b (1); "upper" as the last argument: of a value above x
unifinv||1|3|unifinv(p, a, b): the place where the probability p is reached of the uniform distribution from a (0) to b (1)
unifrnd||0|2|unifrnd(a, b): a random number of the uniform distribution from a (0) to b (1)
unifstat||0|2|unifstat(a, b): [mean, variance] of the uniform distribution from a (0) to b (1)
exppdf||1|2|exppdf(x, mu): density or probability at x of the exponential distribution with the mean mu (1)
expcdf||1|3|expcdf(x, mu): probability of a value up to x of the exponential distribution with the mean mu (1); "upper" as the last argument: of a value above x
expinv||1|2|expinv(p, mu): the place where the probability p is reached of the exponential distribution with the mean mu (1)
exprnd||0|1|exprnd(mu): a random number of the exponential distribution with the mean mu (1)
expstat||0|1|expstat(mu): [mean, variance] of the exponential distribution with the mean mu (1)
gampdf||2|3|gampdf(x, a, b): density or probability at x of the gamma distribution with the shape a and the scale b (1)
gamcdf||2|4|gamcdf(x, a, b): probability of a value up to x of the gamma distribution with the shape a and the scale b (1); "upper" as the last argument: of a value above x
gaminv||2|3|gaminv(p, a, b): the place where the probability p is reached of the gamma distribution with the shape a and the scale b (1)
gamrnd||1|2|gamrnd(a, b): a random number of the gamma distribution with the shape a and the scale b (1)
gamstat||1|2|gamstat(a, b): [mean, variance] of the gamma distribution with the shape a and the scale b (1)
betapdf||3|3|betapdf(x, a, b): density or probability at x of the beta distribution
betacdf||3|4|betacdf(x, a, b): probability of a value up to x of the beta distribution; "upper" as the last argument: of a value above x
betainv||3|3|betainv(p, a, b): the place where the probability p is reached of the beta distribution
betarnd||2|2|betarnd(a, b): a random number of the beta distribution
betastat||2|2|betastat(a, b): [mean, variance] of the beta distribution
chi2pdf||2|2|chi2pdf(x, v): density or probability at x of the chi-square distribution with v degrees of freedom
chi2cdf||2|3|chi2cdf(x, v): probability of a value up to x of the chi-square distribution with v degrees of freedom; "upper" as the last argument: of a value above x
chi2inv||2|2|chi2inv(p, v): the place where the probability p is reached of the chi-square distribution with v degrees of freedom
chi2rnd||1|1|chi2rnd(v): a random number of the chi-square distribution with v degrees of freedom
chi2stat||1|1|chi2stat(v): [mean, variance] of the chi-square distribution with v degrees of freedom
tpdf||2|2|tpdf(x, v): density or probability at x of Student's t distribution with v degrees of freedom
tcdf||2|3|tcdf(x, v): probability of a value up to x of Student's t distribution with v degrees of freedom; "upper" as the last argument: of a value above x
tinv||2|2|tinv(p, v): the place where the probability p is reached of Student's t distribution with v degrees of freedom
trnd||1|1|trnd(v): a random number of Student's t distribution with v degrees of freedom
tstat||1|1|tstat(v): [mean, variance] of Student's t distribution with v degrees of freedom
fpdf||3|3|fpdf(x, v1, v2): density or probability at x of the F distribution with v1 and v2 degrees of freedom
fcdf||3|4|fcdf(x, v1, v2): probability of a value up to x of the F distribution with v1 and v2 degrees of freedom; "upper" as the last argument: of a value above x
frnd||2|2|frnd(v1, v2): a random number of the F distribution with v1 and v2 degrees of freedom
fstat||2|2|fstat(v1, v2): [mean, variance] of the F distribution with v1 and v2 degrees of freedom
lognpdf||1|3|lognpdf(x, mu, sigma): density or probability at x of the lognormal distribution: the logarithm is normal with mu (0) and sigma (1)
logncdf||1|4|logncdf(x, mu, sigma): probability of a value up to x of the lognormal distribution: the logarithm is normal with mu (0) and sigma (1); "upper" as the last argument: of a value above x
logninv||1|3|logninv(p, mu, sigma): the place where the probability p is reached of the lognormal distribution: the logarithm is normal with mu (0) and sigma (1)
lognrnd||0|2|lognrnd(mu, sigma): a random number of the lognormal distribution: the logarithm is normal with mu (0) and sigma (1)
lognstat||0|2|lognstat(mu, sigma): [mean, variance] of the lognormal distribution: the logarithm is normal with mu (0) and sigma (1)
wblpdf||1|3|wblpdf(x, a, b): density or probability at x of the Weibull distribution with the scale a (1) and the shape b (1)
wblcdf||1|4|wblcdf(x, a, b): probability of a value up to x of the Weibull distribution with the scale a (1) and the shape b (1); "upper" as the last argument: of a value above x
wblinv||1|3|wblinv(p, a, b): the place where the probability p is reached of the Weibull distribution with the scale a (1) and the shape b (1)
wblrnd||0|2|wblrnd(a, b): a random number of the Weibull distribution with the scale a (1) and the shape b (1)
wblstat||0|2|wblstat(a, b): [mean, variance] of the Weibull distribution with the scale a (1) and the shape b (1)
raylpdf||2|2|raylpdf(x, b): density or probability at x of the Rayleigh distribution with the scale b
raylcdf||2|3|raylcdf(x, b): probability of a value up to x of the Rayleigh distribution with the scale b; "upper" as the last argument: of a value above x
raylinv||2|2|raylinv(p, b): the place where the probability p is reached of the Rayleigh distribution with the scale b
raylrnd||1|1|raylrnd(b): a random number of the Rayleigh distribution with the scale b
raylstat||1|1|raylstat(b): [mean, variance] of the Rayleigh distribution with the scale b
binopdf||3|3|binopdf(x, n, p): density or probability at x of the binomial distribution: successes in n trials of probability p
binocdf||3|4|binocdf(x, n, p): probability of a value up to x of the binomial distribution: successes in n trials of probability p; "upper" as the last argument: of a value above x
binoinv||3|3|binoinv(p, n, p): the place where the probability p is reached of the binomial distribution: successes in n trials of probability p
binornd||2|2|binornd(n, p): a random number of the binomial distribution: successes in n trials of probability p
binostat||2|2|binostat(n, p): [mean, variance] of the binomial distribution: successes in n trials of probability p
poisspdf||2|2|poisspdf(x, lambda): density or probability at x of the Poisson distribution with the mean lambda
poisscdf||2|3|poisscdf(x, lambda): probability of a value up to x of the Poisson distribution with the mean lambda; "upper" as the last argument: of a value above x
poissinv||2|2|poissinv(p, lambda): the place where the probability p is reached of the Poisson distribution with the mean lambda
poissrnd||1|1|poissrnd(lambda): a random number of the Poisson distribution with the mean lambda
poissstat||1|1|poissstat(lambda): [mean, variance] of the Poisson distribution with the mean lambda
geopdf||2|2|geopdf(x, p): density or probability at x of the geometric distribution: failures before the first success of probability p
geocdf||2|3|geocdf(x, p): probability of a value up to x of the geometric distribution: failures before the first success of probability p; "upper" as the last argument: of a value above x
geoinv||2|2|geoinv(p, p): the place where the probability p is reached of the geometric distribution: failures before the first success of probability p
geornd||1|1|geornd(p): a random number of the geometric distribution: failures before the first success of probability p
geostat||1|1|geostat(p): [mean, variance] of the geometric distribution: failures before the first success of probability p
nbinpdf||3|3|nbinpdf(x, r, p): density or probability at x of the negative binomial distribution: failures before the r-th success
nbincdf||3|4|nbincdf(x, r, p): probability of a value up to x of the negative binomial distribution: failures before the r-th success; "upper" as the last argument: of a value above x
nbininv||3|3|nbininv(p, r, p): the place where the probability p is reached of the negative binomial distribution: failures before the r-th success
nbinrnd||2|2|nbinrnd(r, p): a random number of the negative binomial distribution: failures before the r-th success
nbinstat||2|2|nbinstat(r, p): [mean, variance] of the negative binomial distribution: failures before the r-th success
hygepdf||4|4|hygepdf(x, m, k, n): density or probability at x of the hypergeometric distribution: marked things among n drawn from m of which k are marked
hygecdf||4|5|hygecdf(x, m, k, n): probability of a value up to x of the hypergeometric distribution: marked things among n drawn from m of which k are marked; "upper" as the last argument: of a value above x
hygeinv||4|4|hygeinv(p, m, k, n): the place where the probability p is reached of the hypergeometric distribution: marked things among n drawn from m of which k are marked
hygernd||3|3|hygernd(m, k, n): a random number of the hypergeometric distribution: marked things among n drawn from m of which k are marked
hygestat||3|3|hygestat(m, k, n): [mean, variance] of the hypergeometric distribution: marked things among n drawn from m of which k are marked
unidpdf||2|2|unidpdf(x, n): density or probability at x of the whole numbers from 1 to n, equally likely
unidcdf||2|3|unidcdf(x, n): probability of a value up to x of the whole numbers from 1 to n, equally likely; "upper" as the last argument: of a value above x
unidinv||2|2|unidinv(p, n): the place where the probability p is reached of the whole numbers from 1 to n, equally likely
unidrnd||1|1|unidrnd(n): a random number of the whole numbers from 1 to n, equally likely
unidstat||1|1|unidstat(n): [mean, variance] of the whole numbers from 1 to n, equally likely

# random numbers
rng||0|1|rng(seed): starts the random numbers anew; rng(0) or rng("default"): the stream that MATLAB starts with; rng("shuffle") or rng(): from the clock
rand|randomreal|0|2|a random number from 0 to under 1; rand(n): an n by n matrix; rand(m, n)
randn||0|2|a random number of the standard normal distribution; randn(n), randn(m, n). After rng(seed) the numbers are those of MATLAB's randn (its ziggurat of 256 layers on the twister), as rand and randi are
randi|randominteger|1|3|randi(n): a random whole number from 1 to n; randi([a, b]): from a to b; randi(n, m, k): an m by k matrix
randperm||1|2|randperm(n): the numbers from 1 to n in a random order; randperm(n, k): k of them. After rng(seed) the order is that of MATLAB's randperm (the order that sorts n numbers of the stream)
randsample||2|2|randsample(n, k) or randsample(list, k): k entries taken at random, none twice
shuffle||1|1|a list in a random order
random||1|*|random(dist): a random number of a distribution; random(dist, n): n of them; random("Normal", mu, sigma): by the name MATLAB gives it

# optimisation with exact numbers
linprog||3|7|linprog(f, A, b): the x that makes f*x least with A*x <= b; linprog(f, A, b, Aeq, beq): and Aeq*x = beq; linprog(f, A, b, Aeq, beq, lb, ub): and lb <= x <= ub; exact, by the simplex method; [] leaves a part out
intlinprog||4|8|intlinprog(f, whole, A, b, Aeq, beq, lb, ub): as linprog, with the unknowns numbered in `whole` whole numbers
lsqnonneg||2|2|lsqnonneg(C, d): the x without negative entries that makes the length of C*x - d least

# zeros, minima, integrals and differential equations of functions
!pdf|2|*|pdf(dist, x): density of a distribution at x, or the probability of the value x; pdf("Normal", x, mu, sigma): by the name MATLAB gives it
!cdf|2|*|cdf(dist, x): probability of a value up to x; cdf("Normal", x, mu, sigma)
!quantile|2|*|quantile(list, q): the value at the fraction q of the sorted data, on a straight line between two entries; quantile(dist, q): the place where a distribution reaches the probability q; icdf("Normal", q, mu, sigma)
fzero||2|2|fzero(f, start): a zero of a real function near the start; fzero(f, [a, b]): between two ends where the signs differ; f is an expression, @(x) expr, x -> expr or the name of a function
fminbnd||3|3|fminbnd(f, a, b): the place between a and b where a real function is least
fminsearch|fminunc|2|2|fminsearch(f, start): a place near the start where a function of one or more real numbers is least: fminsearch(@(x, y) ..., [x0, y0])
lsqcurvefit||4|4|lsqcurvefit(@(a, b, x) model, [a0, b0], [x values], [y values]): the parameters with which the model fits the points best
quad|quadgk quadl quadv|3|3|quad(f, a, b): the integral of a function from a to b
integral2|quad2d dblquad|5|5|integral2(f, xa, xb, ya, yb): the integral of f(x, y); ya and yb may be functions of x
integral3|triplequad|7|7|integral3(f, xa, xb, ya, yb, za, zb): the integral of f(x, y, z)
ode45|ode23 ode113 ode78 ode89 ode15s ode23s ode23t ode23tb|3|3|ode45(@(t, y) slope, [t0, t1], y0): the rows [t, y] of the solution of y' = slope at ten equal steps; with more times in the list at those; ode45(@(t, x, y) [slopes], [t0, t1], [x0, y0]) for several unknowns
fsolve||2|2|fsolve(f, start): a zero of a function, or of a list of functions of as many unknowns, near the start
feval||1|*|feval(f, x, ...): the value of a function at its arguments
bsxfun||3|3|bsxfun(f, a, b): f of the entries of two arrays, pair by pair; a number goes with every entry
funm||2|2|funm(A, f): the function f of the matrix A
lsqminnorm||2|2|lsqminnorm(A, b): the shortest x that makes the length of A*x - b least
str2num|str2sym|1|1|the value of the expression that a text writes
str2double||1|1|the number that a text writes, undefined when it writes none
finverse||1|2|finverse(f, x): the inverse function of f, in x
compose||2|5|compose(f, g): f of g; compose(f, g, z): in the variable z; compose(f, g, x, z), compose(f, g, x, y, z): with the variables of f and g named
symvar||1|2|the names of an expression; symvar(expr, n): the n names nearest to x
has||2|2|has(expr, part): whether the part stands in the expression
children||1|1|the parts of an expression: the terms of a sum, the factors of a product, the arguments of a function
lhs||1|1|the left side of an equation
rhs||1|1|the right side of an equation
isalways||1|1|whether a condition holds for all values of its names, as far as can be shown
combine||1|2|combine(expr): powers put together; combine(expr, "sincos"): products of sines and cosines as sums; combine(expr, "log"): logarithms of positive things as one
rewrite||2|2|rewrite(expr, target): in terms of "exp", "sincos", "sinhcosh", "tan", "sin", "cos", "log", "heaviside", "piecewise", "gamma", "factorial", "erf", "sqrt"
potential||2|3|potential([field], [variables]): the function whose gradient the field is, undefined when there is none; potential(field, variables, [base point]): zero there
vectorpotential||2|2|vectorpotential([field], [x, y, z]): a field whose curl the field is, undefined when there is none
equationstomatrix||1|2|equationstomatrix([equations], [variables]): [A, b] with A*variables = b
eliminate||2|2|eliminate([equations], [variables]): equations that hold without those variables
isolate||2|2|isolate(equation, expr): the equation solved for the expression
assumptions||0|0|what is assumed about the names
ttest||1|2|ttest(x): [h, p] of the t test that the mean is 0; ttest(x, m): that it is m; ttest(x, y): of the paired differences; h is 1 when p is under 5%
ttest2||2|2|ttest2(x, y): [h, p] of the t test that two samples have one mean (with one variance)
ztest||3|3|ztest(x, m, sigma): [h, p] of the z test that the mean is m when the deviation sigma is known
vartest||2|2|vartest(x, v): [h, p] of the chi-square test that the variance is v
vartest2||2|2|vartest2(x, y): [h, p] of the F test that two samples have one variance
splitapply||3|3|splitapply(f, values, groups): f of the values of every group
assert||1|2|assert(condition): true, or an error when the condition does not hold; assert(condition, text): with that text
error||1|*|error(text): stops with the text as the error; error(format, values) as sprintf
+map|arrayfun cellfun
+nsolve|vpasolve
+N|vpa
+sum|symsum
+product|symprod
+subst|subs
+together|simplifyfraction
+apart|partfrac
+ztransform|ztrans
+invztransform|iztrans

# data with gaps and outliers, groups
!correlation|2|4|correlation(x, y): Pearson's coefficient of two lists; corr(x, y, "spearman"): of their ranks; corr(x, y, "kendall"): Kendall's tau
+correlation|corr
isoutlier||1|2|for every entry whether it lies far out: more than three scaled median distances from the median; isoutlier(x, "mean"): three deviations from the mean; isoutlier(x, "quartiles"): 1.5 times the middle half beyond it
rmoutliers||1|2|a list without the entries that isoutlier marks
ismissing||1|1|for every entry whether it is undefined
rmmissing||1|1|a list without its undefined entries, a matrix without the rows that have one
fillmissing||2|3|fillmissing(x, "previous" | "next" | "nearest" | "linear"): the undefined entries filled from their neighbours; fillmissing(x, "constant", v): with v
discretize||2|2|discretize(x, edges): the number of the bin between two edges that every entry falls in
findgroups||1|1|for every entry the number of its value among the distinct values in rising order
ecdf||1|1|[values, shares]: the share of the data up to every distinct value
cplxpair||1|1|complex numbers sorted into pairs of conjugates, the real ones last
regress||2|2|regress(y, X): the coefficients b that make the length of X*b - y least

# more on graphs
+components|conncomp
+spanningtree|minspantree
+distancematrix|distances
+vertexcount|numnodes
+edgecount|numedges
+adjacencymatrix|adjacency
+incidencematrix|incidence
+indegrees|indegree
+outdegrees|outdegree
bfsearch||2|2|bfsearch(graph, v): the vertices in the order in which a search by breadth from v reaches them
dfsearch||2|2|dfsearch(graph, v): the vertices in the order in which a search by depth from v reaches them
successors||2|2|successors(graph, v): the vertices that the edges from v lead to
predecessors||2|2|predecessors(graph, v): the vertices with an edge to v
nearest||3|3|nearest(graph, v, d): the vertices within the distance d of v, the nearest first
allpaths||3|3|allpaths(graph, a, b): all ways from a to b that pass no vertex twice
hascycles||1|1|whether a graph has a cycle
isdag||1|1|whether a graph has directions and no cycle
transclosure||1|1|the graph with an arrow from every vertex to every vertex that it reaches
transreduction||1|1|the fewest arrows of a graph without cycles that keep what reaches what
biconncomp||1|1|the parts of a graph that stay in one piece when any one vertex goes
cutvertices|articulationvertices|1|1|the vertices whose removal splits a graph
bridges||1|1|the edges whose removal splits a graph
centrality||2|2|centrality(graph, kind): how central every vertex is: "degree", "indegree", "outdegree", "closeness", "incloseness", "outcloseness", "betweenness", "pagerank", "eigenvector"
addedge||3|4|addedge(graph, a, b): the graph with one more edge; addedge(graph, a, b, w): with the weight w
rmedge||3|3|rmedge(graph, a, b): the graph without the edges between a and b
addnode||2|2|addnode(graph, v): the graph with one more vertex
rmnode||2|2|rmnode(graph, v): the graph without a vertex and its edges
findedge||3|3|findedge(graph, a, b): the number of the edge between a and b in the list, 0 when there is none
flipedge||1|1|a graph with every arrow turned round

# geometry of points
polyarea||1|2|polyarea([x values], [y values]) or polyarea([[x, y], ...]): the area of the polygon with these corners
inpolygon||4|4|inpolygon(x, y, [corner x values], [corner y values]): whether a point lies in a polygon or on its edge; lists of points give lists
convhull||1|2|convhull([x values], [y values]): the numbers of the points at the corners of the convex hull, against the clock, the first again at the end
convexhull||1|2|the corners of the convex hull of points, against the clock
delaunay||1|2|delaunay([x values], [y values]): the triangles of Delaunay as triples of the numbers of the points
voronoi||1|2|voronoi([x values], [y values]): the corners of the Voronoi cells of the points
rectint||2|2|rectint([x, y, w, h], [x, y, w, h]): the area that two rectangles share
dsearchn||2|2|dsearchn([points], point): the number of the nearest of the points; a list of points gives a list
rotx||1|1|the matrix of the turn about the x axis by an angle in degrees
roty||1|1|the matrix of the turn about the y axis by an angle in degrees
rotz||1|1|the matrix of the turn about the z axis by an angle in degrees

# kinds of whole numbers, values of the largest size, the session
int8||1|1|rounded to a whole number from -128 to 127
int16||1|1|rounded to a whole number from -32768 to 32767
int32||1|1|rounded to a whole number from -2^31 to 2^31 - 1
int64||1|1|rounded to a whole number from -2^63 to 2^63 - 1
uint8||1|1|rounded to a whole number from 0 to 255
uint16||1|1|rounded to a whole number from 0 to 65535
uint32||1|1|rounded to a whole number from 0 to 2^32 - 1
uint64||1|1|rounded to a whole number from 0 to 2^64 - 1
eigs||1|2|eigs(A, k): the k eigenvalues of the largest size (6 without k)
svds||1|2|svds(A, k): the k largest singular values (6 without k)
disp||1|1|disp(x): shows a value: a text without quotes, a matrix row by row
fprintf||1|*|fprintf(format, values): shows the text that sprintf makes
readmatrix|csvread dlmread|1|1|readmatrix("file.csv"): the matrix of the numbers of a file: lines of numbers with commas, semicolons, tabs or blanks between them
writematrix|csvwrite dlmwrite|2|2|writematrix(A, "file.csv"): writes a matrix as lines of numbers with commas between them
tic||0|0|starts the clock of toc
toc||0|0|the seconds since tic()
who||0|0|the names that have a value or are defined as functions
clear||0|*|clear(): forgets all names that were given values or defined; clear(x, f): those names

# charts of data (they take what is chosen for a picture, and save_png / save_jxl save them)
lineplot|dataplot|1|*|lineplot([y values]), lineplot([x values], [y values]) or lineplot([[x, y], ...]): a line through points
scatter||1|*|scatter([x values], [y values]): points as dots
bar||1|*|bar([values]) or bar([x values], [values]): upright bars
barh||1|*|barh([values]): bars that lie
stairs||1|*|stairs([x values], [y values]): steps: every value holds until the next x
stem||1|*|stem([x values], [y values]): a stalk with a dot for every value
errorbar||2|*|errorbar([x values], [y values], [errors]): points with bars of their errors
pie||1|*|pie([values]) or pie([values], ["names"]): the shares of a sum as pieces of a circle
loglog||1|*|loglog([x values], [y values]): a line through points, both axes by logarithms
semilogx||1|*|semilogx([x values], [y values]): a line through points, the x axis by logarithms
semilogy||1|*|semilogy([x values], [y values]): a line through points, the y axis by logarithms
contour|fcontour|1|*|contour(f(x, y), x, a, b, y, c, d[, heights]) or contour(matrix[, heights]): lines of equal height
quiver||4|*|quiver([u(x, y), v(x, y)], x, a, b, y, c, d) or quiver([x], [y], [u], [v]): the arrows of a field
plot3||1|*|plot3([x values], [y values], [z values]) or plot3([[x, y, z], ...]): a line through points of space
scatter3||1|*|scatter3([x values], [y values], [z values]): points of space
surf|mesh|1|*|surf(matrix of heights) or surf([x values], [y values], matrix): a surface over a grid
spy||1|*|spy(matrix): where the entries of a matrix are not zero
boxplot||1|*|boxplot([values]) or boxplot([[values], ...]): boxes of the quartiles with whiskers and outliers
imread||1|2|imread("picture.png"): the samples of a PNG or JPEG XL picture as a matrix (gray) or as a list of the matrices of its channels; imread("moving.jxl", 3): of its third frame
imframes||1|1|imframes("moving.jxl"): how many frames a PNG or JPEG XL picture has
imwrite||2|3|imwrite(A, "picture.png"[, bits]): writes a matrix of numbers from 0 to 1 (or whole numbers up to 255 or 65535) as a gray picture, a list of 3 or 4 matrices as RGB or RGBA
+matrixplot|imagesc imshow pcolor image
+plot|fplot ezplot
+plot3d|fsurf fmesh ezsurf
+implicitplot|fimplicit
+parametricplot3d|fplot3
+densityplot|contourf

# more of MATLAB: arrays of more sizes, matrices by the places of their entries, data with gaps
!reshape|2|3|
+intersection|intersect
+complement|setdiff
+assume|assumealso
+nintegrate|vpaintegral
+strsplit|split
+fft2|fftn
+ifft2|ifftn
+conv2|convn
condest||1|1|the condition number of a matrix in the 1-norm, exactly
normest||1|1|the largest singular value of a matrix, exactly
filter2||2|3|filter2(h, X): the matrix X filtered with the weights h, as large as X; filter2(h, X, "full"), filter2(h, X, "valid")
newline||0|0|the text of a new line
meanabs||1|1|the mean of the absolute values of the entries
nansum||1|2|the sum of the entries that are not undefined; of a matrix down its columns; nansum(A, 2): along its rows
nanmean||1|2|the mean of the entries that are not undefined
nanmax||1|2|the largest of the entries that are not undefined
nanmin||1|2|the smallest of the entries that are not undefined
nanstd||1|2|the standard deviation of the entries that are not undefined
nanvar||1|2|the variance of the entries that are not undefined
nanmedian||1|2|the median of the entries that are not undefined
single||1|1|the nearest number with 24 bits, as MATLAB's single numbers are
cast||2|2|cast(x, "int8"): a value as the kind named: int8 to uint64, single, double, logical, char
class||1|1|the kind of a value by the names of MATLAB: "double", "char", "logical", "sym", "function_handle", "cell"
isa||2|2|isa(x, kind): whether a value is of a kind: those of class, "numeric", "float"
sparse||1|5|sparse(i, j, values): the matrix with the values at the rows i and the columns j, the values at one place added; sparse(i, j, values, rows, columns); sparse(rows, columns): zeros; sparse(A): A itself
full||1|1|a matrix itself (all matrices are full here)
issparse||1|1|false: all matrices are full here
nzmax||1|1|the number of entries that are not zero
spones||1|1|1 where an entry is not zero, 0 elsewhere
speye||1|2|the identity matrix; speye(rows, columns)
spdiags||4|4|spdiags(B, d, rows, columns): the matrix with the columns of B along the diagonals d
squeeze||1|1|an array of three or more sizes without the sizes that are 1
permute||2|2|permute(array, order): the sizes of an array in another order: permute(A, [2, 1]) is the transpose
ipermute||2|2|ipermute(array, order): what permute with that order turned
num2cell||1|1|num2cell(list): the cell array that holds every entry of a list in a cell of its own
cell2mat||1|1|cell2mat({matrices}): the matrices that a cell array (or a list) holds as one matrix, side by side; rows of matrices as blocks
signim||1|1|the sign of the imaginary part of a number; of a real number the opposite of its sign
padecoef||1|2|padecoef(T, n): [numerator, denominator] of Pade's fraction of order n (1 without n) for e^(-T*s), the highest power first
crosstab||2|2|crosstab(x, y): how often every value of x comes with every value of y
pdist||1|1|the distances between the rows of a matrix: 1 and 2, 1 and 3, ..., 2 and 3, ...
squareform||1|1|the distances of pdist as a symmetric matrix, or such a matrix as the list
normfit||1|1|[mean, standard deviation] of data
expfit||1|1|the mean of data: the scale of the exponential distribution that fits it
partialcorr||1|1|the correlations of every two columns of a matrix with the other columns held fixed
del2||1|2|del2(U): the discrete Laplacian of a list or a matrix over 4, the ends on straight lines; del2(U, h): for the step h
interpft||2|2|interpft(x, n): n values of the waves through the entries of a list, at equal steps
datasample||2|2|datasample(list, k): k entries taken at random, some perhaps more than once
quadprog||2|8|quadprog(H, f, A, b, Aeq, beq, lb, ub): the x that makes x'*H*x/2 + f*x least with A*x <= b, Aeq*x = beq, lb <= x <= ub, for an H without negative eigenvalues; exact
lsqlin||2|8|lsqlin(C, d, A, b, Aeq, beq, lb, ub): the x that makes the length of C*x - d least with A*x <= b, Aeq*x = beq, lb <= x <= ub; exact
lsqnonlin||2|2|lsqnonlin(@(a, b) [expressions], [start of a, of b]): the a and b that make the sum of the squares of the expressions least
mellin||3|3|mellin(f, x, s): the Mellin transform, the integral of x^(s - 1)*f(x) from 0 on
anova1||1|1|anova1([[values of a group], ...]): the probability that groups with one mean differ as much as these (analysis of variance)
polyeig||2|*|polyeig(A0, A1, ..., Ap): the numbers x with det(A0 + x*A1 + ... + x^p*Ap) = 0
pca||1|1|pca(X): [directions, scores, variances] of the principal components of data with an observation in every row, in decimals
mkpp||2|2|mkpp(breaks, coefficients): pieces of polynomials: between two breaks the polynomial with a row of coefficients, the highest first, in the distance from the break before
unmkpp||1|1|[breaks, coefficients, number of pieces, number of coefficients, 1] of pieces of polynomials
ppval||2|2|ppval(pieces, x): the value of pieces of polynomials at x; the first and the last piece go on outside
polynomialreduce||2|3|polynomialreduce(p, [divisors], [variables]): what is left of p after division by the divisors
griddata||5|6|griddata(x, y, values, xq, yq): the value at a point between scattered points, on the triangles of Delaunay; "nearest" as a sixth argument: of the nearest point
tsearchn||3|3|tsearchn([points], [triangles], point): the number of the triangle that holds a point, undefined when none does
erase||2|2|erase(text, part): the text without the part, wherever it stands
extractbefore||2|2|extractbefore(text, part): what stands before the part; extractbefore(text, n): before character n
extractafter||2|2|extractafter(text, part): what stands after the part; extractafter(text, n): after character n
extractbetween||3|3|extractbetween(text, start, end): what stands between two parts (a list when it does so more than once); with two numbers: the characters from one to the other
insertafter||3|3|insertafter(text, part, new): the new text after every such part; insertafter(text, n, new): after character n
insertbefore||3|3|insertbefore(text, part, new): the new text before every such part; insertbefore(text, n, new): before character n
pad||2|4|pad(text, n): the text with blanks after it up to n characters; pad(text, n, "left" | "both"); pad(text, n, "left", "0"): with another character
isstrprop||2|2|isstrprop(text, kind): for every character whether it is of a kind: "alpha", "digit", "alphanum", "upper", "lower", "wspace", "punct", "xdigit"
!spline|2|3|spline(x, y, q): the value at q of the cubic spline through the points (smooth to the second derivative); spline(x, y): its pieces for ppval
!pchip|2|3|pchip(x, y, q): the value at q of the cubic pieces through the points that keep the shape of the data; pchip(x, y): the pieces for ppval
!makima|2|3|makima(x, y, q): the value at q of Akima's cubic pieces through the points, without overshoot; makima(x, y): the pieces for ppval

# more charts, and pictures as matrices
stem3||1|*|stem3([x values], [y values], [z values]): a stalk from the plane z = 0 to every point of space
quiver3||6|*|quiver3([x], [y], [z], [u], [v], [w]): an arrow by (u, v, w) from every point (x, y, z)
contour3||1|*|contour3(f(x, y), x, a, b, y, c, d[, heights]) or contour3(matrix[, heights]): the lines of equal height, each at its height in space
waterfall||1|*|waterfall(matrix) or waterfall([x values], [y values], matrix): the rows of a matrix as lines of space, one behind the other
compass||1|*|compass([u values], [v values]) or compass([complex numbers]): arrows from the origin
feather||1|*|feather([u values], [v values]) or feather([complex numbers]): arrows from the places 1, 2, 3 ... on the x axis
polarscatter||2|*|polarscatter([angles], [radii]): points at their angles (in radians) and distances around the origin
rgb2gray||1|1|rgb2gray([R, G, B]): the gray picture 0.2989*R + 0.587*G + 0.114*B of the matrices of three colours; whole samples stay whole
im2double||1|2|im2double(A): the samples 0 to 255 of a picture as shares from 0 to 1; im2double(A, 16): of 16 bits
im2uint8||1|1|the shares from 0 to 1 of a picture as whole samples from 0 to 255
im2uint16||1|1|the shares from 0 to 1 of a picture as whole samples from 0 to 65535
imcomplement||1|1|the negative of a picture: 1 - x for shares, 255 - x (or 65535 - x) for samples
imcrop||2|2|imcrop(A, [x, y, width, height]): the columns from x to x + width and the rows from y to y + height of a picture
imresize||2|3|imresize(A, scale) or imresize(A, [rows, columns]): a picture of another size, by straight lines between the middles of the pixels (nothing is smoothed first); imresize(A, s, "nearest"): the nearest pixel
imrotate||2|2|imrotate(A, degrees): a picture turned against the clock about its middle: quarter turns exactly, other angles by the nearest pixel in a picture as large as it has to be
imfilter||2|3|imfilter(A, h): a picture with the weights h laid on every pixel, as large as it was, zeros around it; imfilter(A, h, "conv"): the weights turned round
imhist||1|2|imhist(A): how many pixels have every sample from 0 to 255; imhist(A, bits): of that many bits
imbinarize||1|2|imbinarize(A, level): 1 where a pixel is above the level, else 0; without a level: the middle between the darkest and the lightest pixel
+rescale|mat2gray
htrans||3|3|htrans(f, x, t): the Hilbert transform of f(x), the integral of f(x)/(t - x) over all x, over pi: of waves, of 1/(x^2 + c), x/(x^2 + c), sin(x)/x, 1/x and diracdelta
ihtrans||3|3|ihtrans(f, x, t): the function whose Hilbert transform f is
# the calendar
datenum|datetime|1|6|datenum(year, month, day) with or without (hour, minute, second), datenum([year, month, day]), datenum("2026-11-20 13:45:10"): the days since the year 0 began, as MATLAB counts them; datenum(text, "dd.mm.yyyy") reads another form; datenum(number, "juliandate" | "modifiedjuliandate" | "posixtime" | "excel" | "yyyymmdd")
datevec||1|2|datevec(date): [year, month, day, hour, minute, second] of a day number or of a date in quotes; datevec(text, form)
datestr||1|2|datestr(date): the date in words, as "20-Nov-2026 13:45:10"; datestr(date, form): with yyyy yy mmmm mmm mm m dddd ddd dd d HH MM SS FFF AM PM QQ, or one of MATLAB's forms 0 to 31
weekday||1|2|weekday(date): the day of the week, 1 for Sunday to 7 for Saturday; weekday(date, "long" | "short"): its name
eomday||2|2|eomday(year, month): the last day of the month
calendar||0|2|calendar(year, month), calendar(date): the days of the month in six weeks that begin on Sunday, 0 where the month has no day; calendar(): of this month
addtodate||3|3|addtodate(date, number, "year" | "month" | "day" | "hour" | "minute" | "second" | "millisecond"): the day number moved; with years and months the day stays within its month
etime||2|2|etime(later, sooner): the seconds between two dates [year, month, day, hour, minute, second]
juliandate||1|1|juliandate(date): the Julian date of a day number, a date in quotes or [year, month, day]
mjuliandate|modifiedjuliandate|1|1|mjuliandate(date): the modified Julian date, the Julian date less 2400000.5
posixtime||1|1|posixtime(date): the seconds since 1970 began
dayofyear||1|1|dayofyear(date): the place of the day in its year, from 1
isweekend||1|1|isweekend(date): whether the day is a Saturday or a Sunday
leapyear|isleapyear is_leap_year|1|1|leapyear(year): whether the year has a 29th of February
yeardays||1|1|yeardays(year): the days of the year, 365 or 366
now||0|0|now(): this moment as a day number, by the clock of this place
clock||0|0|clock(): this moment as [year, month, day, hour, minute, second]
date|today|0|0|date(): this day in words, as "20-Nov-2026"
cputime||0|0|cputime(): the seconds for which this run has kept a processor busy
# patterns of texts
regexp||2|*|regexp(text, pattern): the places where the pattern begins in the text; with "match", "tokens", "names", "start", "end", "split", "tokenextents": the matches, their parts in brackets, ..., several of them as a list in that order; "once": the first match only; "ignorecase", "emptymatch", "lineanchors", "dotexceptnewline". A pattern has . [a-z] [^a-z] \d \w \s \D \W \S, * + ? {2,5} with ? for the fewest and + for no way back, ( ) (?: ) (?<name> ) |, ^ $ \< \>, (?= ) (?! ) (?<= ) (?<! ) (?> ), \1 \k<name>, (?i)
regexpi||2|*|regexpi(text, pattern, ...): regexp that takes capitals for small letters
regexprep||3|*|regexprep(text, pattern, new): the text with every match replaced; in the new text $0 is the match, $1 its first part in brackets, $<name> a part by its name; "once", "ignorecase", or the number of the match to replace; lists of patterns are replaced one after the other
regexptranslate||2|2|regexptranslate("escape", text): the pattern that matches just the text; regexptranslate("wildcard", text): with * for any signs and ? for one
strjust||1|2|strjust(text, "right" | "left" | "center"): the blanks at the ends of a text moved to the other side
validatestring||2|2|validatestring(text, [words]): the word that the text is or begins, capitals or not
# tests of ranks and counts, groups of points
ranksum||2|3|ranksum(x, y): [p, h] of Wilcoxon's test that two samples have one median, by the sum of the ranks; every case is counted (p is a fraction) for small samples, or with "exact"; else, or with "approximate", by the normal law
signrank||1|3|signrank(x), signrank(x, y), signrank(x, m): [p, h] of Wilcoxon's test of signed ranks that the median is 0, that of x - y is 0, or is m; "exact" or "approximate" as the last argument
signtest||1|3|signtest(x), signtest(x, y), signtest(x, m): [p, h] of the test of signs that the median is 0, that of x - y is 0, or is m
kruskalwallis||1|1|kruskalwallis([[group], [group], ...]): the probability that groups of one distribution have ranks as far apart
friedman||1|1|friedman(matrix): the probability that columns alike differ as much by their ranks within the rows
vartestn|bartlett|1|1|vartestn([[group], [group], ...]): the probability, by Bartlett's test, that groups of one variance differ as much; bartlett(n) with a whole number: the triangle window of n values with zeros at its ends
fishertest||1|1|fishertest([[a, b], [c, d]]): [h, p, odds ratio] of Fisher's exact test of a table of counts; p is a fraction
chi2gof||2|3|chi2gof(counts, expected): [h, p, statistic] of the chi-square test that counts follow the expected counts or shares; chi2gof(counts, expected, k): with k parameters fitted
kstest||1|2|kstest(x): [h, p, distance] of the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test that a sample is standard normal; kstest(x, distribution): that it has that distribution
kstest2||2|2|kstest2(x, y): [h, p, distance] of the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test that two samples have one distribution
jbtest||1|1|jbtest(x): [h, p, statistic] of the Jarque-Bera test that a sample is normal, by the chi-square law of 2 degrees
anova2||1|2|anova2(matrix): the probabilities [of the columns, of the rows] that such means are alike; anova2(matrix, repeats): every so many rows are one group, [columns, rows, both together]
mahal||2|2|mahal(points, sample): the squared Mahalanobis distance of every point (a row) from a sample of rows
mvnpdf||1|3|mvnpdf(x, mean, covariance): the density of the normal distribution of several coordinates at x, or at every row of x
mvnrnd||2|3|mvnrnd(mean, covariance, count): random points of the normal distribution of several coordinates
ksdensity||1|3|ksdensity(x, points): the density of a sample smoothed with normal bells, at the points; ksdensity(x): [densities, points] at 100 points; ksdensity(x, points, width)
jackknife||2|2|jackknife(f, list): f of the list without its first entry, without its second, ...
bootstrp||3|3|bootstrp(count, f, list): f of so many random samples of the list, drawn with returns
grpstats||2|3|grpstats(values, groups): the mean of the values of every group, the groups in order; grpstats(values, groups, "std" | ["mean", "numel"]): "mean" "std" "var" "sem" "min" "max" "median" "range" "sum" "numel"
linkage||1|2|linkage(points, way): the steps [group, group, distance] of gathering points (rows) into ever larger groups; the points are groups 1 to n, every step makes the next; "single" (default), "complete", "average", "weighted", "centroid", "median", "ward"; a flat list holds the distances of the pairs as pdist gives them
cluster||2|2|cluster(steps, count): the group of every point when the steps of linkage stop at so many groups
kmeans||2|2|kmeans(points, count): [the group of every point, the means of the groups]; starts from the points farthest apart
fitlm||2|2|fitlm(x, y): the straight fit y = b0 + b1*x1 + ... as a record: coefficients (the constant first), se, tstat, pvalue, rsquared, adjusted, rmse, dfe, fstat, p; x is a list or a matrix with a row for every y
# records and tables
struct||0|*|struct(name, value, ...): a record of named fields; getfield, setfield, fieldnames, rmfield, isfield work with it
isstruct||1|1|isstruct(x): whether x is a record
fieldnames||1|1|fieldnames(record): the names of its fields
numfields||1|1|numfields(record): how many fields it has; 0 for what is no record
getfield||2|*|getfield(record, name): the value of a field; getfield(record, name, name): of a field of a record within
setfield||3|*|setfield(record, name, value): the record with a field set or added; setfield(record, name, name, value): in a record within
rmfield||2|2|rmfield(record, name | [names]): the record without those fields
isfield||2|2|isfield(record, name | [names]): whether it has the field
orderfields||1|1|orderfields(record): the fields in the order of their names
struct2cell||1|1|struct2cell(record): the values of its fields, a cell array
cell2struct||2|2|cell2struct({values}, [names]): the record with those fields
structfun||2|2|structfun(f, record): f of the value of every field
dictionary||0|*|dictionary(keys, values), dictionary(key, value, ...): a table that finds a value by its key (a number or a text); keys, values, lookup, insert, remove, iskey work with it
keys||1|1|keys(table): its keys, in the order they came
values||1|2|values(table): its values; values(table, [keys]): those of the keys
entries||1|1|entries(table): [key, value] of every entry
numentries||1|1|numentries(table): how many entries it has
iskey||2|2|iskey(table, key | [keys]): whether the key is there
lookup||2|3|lookup(table, key | [keys]): the value of a key; lookup(table, key, fallback): the fallback when the key is not there
remove||2|2|remove(table, key | [keys]): the table without those keys
iscell||1|1|iscell(x): whether x is a cell array {a, b, ...} (or a list of unlike things or of texts, which stands for one)
iscellstr||1|1|iscellstr(x): whether x is a cell array (or a list) of texts
cell||1|2|cell(n): a cell array of n empty cells; cell(rows, columns): rows of empty cells
cellarray||0|*|cellarray(a, b, ...) or {a, b, ...}: a cell array, a container that holds anything in every cell (numbers, texts, lists, matrices, records, other cell arrays); {a, b; c, d} holds rows of cells. part(c, k) is what the k-th cell holds; the functions of lists take a cell array as the list of what it holds, and those that order or choose (sort, unique, reverse, horzcat, take, upper ...) give a cell array again
celldisp||1|2|celldisp({a, b, ...}[, "name"]): what every cell holds, one text for a cell, the cells of cells too
mat2cell||2|3|mat2cell(matrix, [rows of every block], [columns of every block]): the blocks of a matrix as a cell array (rows of blocks when the columns are cut too)
!insert|3|3|insert(list, x, i): the list with x as entry i; insert(table, key, value): the table with a key set
# more special functions
meijerg||5|5|meijerg([a1, ..., an], [more a], [b1, ..., bm], [more b], z): Meijer's G function, the integral of a quotient of gamma functions that holds most special functions; a number where its series converge
lerchphi|lerch|3|3|lerchphi(z, s, a): Lerch's transcendent, the sum of z^k/(k + a)^s over k from 0; a number inside the unit circle and at z = 1 and z = -1
# expressions in an unknown function and its derivatives
functionalderivative|eulerlagrange|1|3|functionalderivative(f, y, x): the derivative of the functional that integrates f(x, y, y', y'', ...) over x, by its function y; where it is zero the functional is stationary (the equation of Euler and Lagrange)
odetovectorfield||1|3|odetovectorfield(equation, y, x): an equation of order n as the derivatives of [y, y', ..., y^(n-1)], the highest derivative solved for
# the least value under conditions
fmincon||2|9|fmincon(f, start, A, b, Aeq, beq, lower, upper, conditions): the least value of f where conditions hold: A*x <= b, Aeq*x = beq, lower <= x <= upper and conditions(x) = [[values that are <= 0], [values that are 0]]; [] leaves a part out; the point is given
# matrices with names, a surface, colours, counts of pairs
gallery||2|5|gallery(kind, n, ...): a matrix with a name: "minij", "moler" (n, alpha), "lehmer", "fiedler" (n or a list), "frank" (n, k), "tridiag" (n, below, on, above), "ris", "kms" (n, rho), "parter", "pei" (n, alpha), "clement" (n, k), "grcar" (n, k), "jordbloc" (n, lambda), "circul" (a list), "cauchy" (x, y), "lotkin"
peaks||0|2|peaks(n): the surface with three peaks of MATLAB over n by n places from -3 to 3 (49 without n); peaks(x, y): its value at a place
colormap||1|2|colormap(name, count): so many colours of a palette as rows [red, green, blue] from 0 to 1 (256 without a count)
histcounts2||3|4|histcounts2(x, y, cells): how many pairs fall into every cell of a grid, a row for every cell of x; histcounts2(x, y, [cells of x, cells of y]); histcounts2(x, y, edges of x, edges of y)
pcg|gmres bicg bicgstab cgs minres qmr symmlq tfqmr|2|*|pcg(A, b), gmres(A, b), ...: the solution of A*x = b; the methods of MATLAB that come near it step by step give it here at once
lsqr||2|*|lsqr(A, b): the x with the least |A*x - b|
+pie|pie3
+lineplot|comet
+plot3|comet3
# pairs of matrices, forms in another order
balance||1|1|balance(A): [T, B] with B = T^-1*A*T: rows and columns exchanged where that sets an eigenvalue apart, then scaled by powers of two until every row is about as large as its column
qz||2|2|qz(A, B): [AA, BB, Q, Z] with Q*A*Z = AA and Q*B*Z = BB zero below their diagonals and unitary Q and Z (the Schur form of a pair); AA[i][i]/BB[i][i] are the eigenvalues of the pair
ordqz||5|5|ordqz(AA, BB, Q, Z, select): the Schur form of a pair with the chosen eigenvalues first; select as for ordschur
gsvd||2|2|gsvd(A, B): the generalized singular values of two matrices with as many columns, the least first
gsvddecomposition||2|2|gsvddecomposition(A, B): [U, V, X, C, S] with A = U*C*X^H, B = V*S*X^H, unitary U and V and C^H*C + S^H*S = 1
ordschur||3|3|ordschur(U, T, select): [U, T] of a Schur form in another order: the eigenvalues that a list of truths chooses come first; "lhp", "rhp" (the left and the right half of the plane), "udi", "udo" (inside and outside the unit circle); a list of numbers: the greater, the sooner
ordeig||1|2|ordeig(T): the eigenvalues in the order of the diagonal of a Schur form (two for a block of two); ordeig(AA, BB): of a pair
rsf2csf||2|2|rsf2csf(U, T): [U, T] of the complex Schur form (zero below the diagonal) from a real one with blocks of two
cdf2rdf||2|2|cdf2rdf(V, D): [V, D] with real numbers only, from eigenvectors V and a diagonal D with pairs of conjugate numbers: every pair becomes a block of two
# runs and spreads of samples, the normal law of several variables, laws fitted to data
runstest||1|4|runstest(x[, level | "ud"][, "exact" | "approximate"]): [h, p, runs] of the runs of values above and below a level (the mean when none is given; values at the level are left out), with "ud" of the runs up and down (equal neighbours count once); every order is counted (p is a fraction) unless "approximate" asks for the normal law with the count moved half a step towards its mean, which runs up and down of more than 50 values use
ansaribradley||2|3|ansaribradley(x, y[, "exact" | "approximate"]): [h, p, W] of the test that two samples spread alike: W adds the places of x counted from both ends; up to 25 values every choice is counted (p is a fraction), beyond that the normal law
lillietest||1|1|lillietest(x): [h, p, D] of the test of Lilliefors that the values follow a normal law with the mean and the spread of the data; p (four digits) by the formulas of Dallal and Wilkinson and of Stephens
mvncdf||1|4|mvncdf(x), mvncdf(x, mu), mvncdf(x, mu, sigma), mvncdf(lower, upper, mu, sigma): the chance that normal variables with the means mu and the covariances sigma (a matrix, or a list of variances) are all below x, or in a box; x a list or a matrix with a point in every row; up to four variables to the digits of the session (integrals of Plackett's identity), from five by shifted lattices of points (some five digits, as many as the lattices agree in)
cmdscale||1|1|cmdscale(D): [Y, e]: points (in the rows of Y) whose distances are those of the symmetric matrix D (or of the list that pdist gives), from the eigenvalues e of the centred squares; the largest entry of every column of Y is positive
procrustes||2|6|procrustes(X, Y[, "scaling", false][, "reflection", true | false | "best"]): [d, Z, struct("T", T, "b", b, "c", c)]: the points of Y turned, scaled and moved onto those of X: Z = b*Y*T + c, and d the squares of what is left over the squares of X about its middle
canoncorr||2|2|canoncorr(X, Y): [A, B, r, U, V]: the weights A and B of the columns of X and Y whose sums U = (X - mean)*A and V = (Y - mean)*B correlate most, the correlations r from the largest; the largest weight of every column of A is positive
nnmf||2|2|nnmf(A, k): [W, H, D] with W*H near A, all entries zero or more, k columns in W: least squares one factor at a time from the leading singular pairs; rows of H of length 1, columns of W from the longest, D the root of the mean square that is left
poissfit||1|1|poissfit(x): the mean of the Poisson law that makes the counts likeliest
unifit||1|1|unifit(x): [a, b], the ends of the uniform law that makes the data likeliest
raylfit||1|1|raylfit(x): the parameter of the Rayleigh law that makes the data likeliest, sqrt(sum(x^2)/(2n))
lognfit||1|1|lognfit(x): [mu, sigma] of the lognormal law: the mean and the spread (dividing by n - 1) of the logarithms
binofit||2|2|binofit(successes, trials): the chance of success that makes the counts likeliest
gamfit||1|1|gamfit(x): [shape, scale] of the gamma law that makes the data likeliest
wblfit||1|1|wblfit(x): [scale, shape] of the law of Weibull that makes the data likeliest
betafit||1|1|betafit(x): [a, b] of the beta law that makes the data (between 0 and 1) likeliest
nbinfit||1|1|nbinfit(x): [r, p] of the negative binomial law that makes the counts likeliest
mle||1|5|mle(x[, "distribution", name][, "ntrials", n]): the parameters that make the data likeliest, as MATLAB orders them; the normal law without a name (its spread divides by n); normal, exponential, poisson, rayleigh, lognormal, gamma, weibull, beta, uniform, "discrete uniform", geometric, binomial, bernoulli, "negative binomial"
fitdist||2|4|fitdist(x, name[, "ntrials", n]): the law of that name fitted to the data, as a distribution that pdf, cdf, quantile, avg, random take; the names of mle
# values at both ends, equations that look back, heat in one dimension, least values without slopes
bvp4c|bvp5c|3|5|bvp4c(f, conditions, mesh, guess[, parameters]): the functions with the slopes f(x, y, ...) whose values at the two ends of the mesh make the conditions(ya, ..., yb, ...) zero; rows [x, y, ...] at the points of the mesh, to the digits of the session (shooting from many points, the steps of Newton); the guess: numbers, a function of x, or a row for every point; with unknown parameters f(x, y, ..., p) and conditions(ya, ..., yb, ..., p) give [rows, parameters]; bvp4c(f, conditions, bvpinit(mesh, guess))
bvpinit||2|3|bvpinit(mesh, guess[, parameters]): the start of bvp4c as a struct with the fields x, y and parameters
deval||2|3|deval(rows, x): the solution between the rows [t, y, ...] of ode45, bvp4c or dde23 by a spline; deval(rows, x, f): by the slopes f(t, y, ...) themselves from the row before, to all digits
dde23||4|4|dde23(f, lags, history, [t0, t1]): rows [t, y, ...] of the functions with the slopes f(t, y, ..., z, ...), where z, ... are the values of y, ... at t minus the lags (all functions at the first lag, then at the next); the history before t0 is numbers or a function of t; eleven rows, or a row for every time of a longer list
pdepe||6|6|pdepe(m, pde, start, ends, mesh, times): the equation c*u_t = x^-m*(x^m*f)_x + s in one dimension of space (m = 0 a slab, 1 a cylinder, 2 a ball), with pde(x, t, u, ux) giving [c, f, s], start(x) the values at the first time and ends(xl, ul, xr, ur, t) giving [pl, ql, pr, qr] of the conditions p + q*f = 0 at both ends; a matrix with a row for every time and a column for every point of the mesh (cells about the points, second order in the mesh; the times by steps that stiff equations allow, twelve digits); several unknowns: pde(x, t, u, v, ux, vx) gives [[c...], [f...], [s...]] and a matrix comes for each
patternsearch||2|8|patternsearch(f, start[, lower, upper]) or patternsearch(f, start, A, b, [], [], lower, upper): a point where the function is least, sought without slopes (so kinks do no harm): steps along the axes, strides along the way that helped, half the step when nothing helps (Hooke and Jeeves), to the digits of the session
particleswarm||2|8|particleswarm(f, number of unknowns[, lower, upper]): the least point that a swarm of points finds, each drawn towards its own best and the best of all (from -10 to 10 without bounds), then polished as patternsearch does; the numbers drawn are the same every time unless rng gave a seed
simulannealbnd||2|8|simulannealbnd(f, start[, lower, upper]): the least point of a walk that takes worse points too while it is hot and cools slowly, then polished as patternsearch does
ga||2|8|ga(f, number of unknowns[, lower, upper]) or ga(f, number, A, b, [], [], lower, upper): the least point of generations of points: the best stay, the others blend two parents and now and then change by chance; then polished as patternsearch does
globalsearch|multistart|2|8|globalsearch(f, start[, lower, upper]): the least of the points that patternsearch finds from the best of 200 starts drawn in the box (from -10 to 10 without bounds) and from the start
fminimax||2|8|fminimax(@(x, y) [f, g, ...], start[, A, b, Aeq, beq, lower, upper]): the point where the largest of the functions is least (fmincon with one unknown more that bounds them all)
fgoalattain||4|10|fgoalattain(@(x, y) [f, g, ...], start, goals, weights[, A, b, Aeq, beq, lower, upper]): the point where the functions come as far below their goals as can be, each measured by its weight
pdeval||4|4|pdeval(m, mesh, values, x): [u, ux], the values of a row of pdepe at places between the points of the mesh and the slopes there (straight lines between the points, as the cells of pdepe take them)
# tables of named columns, tables with times, values of a few kinds
istable||1|1|istable(x): whether x is a table made by table("name", [values], ...) (a timetable is none)
timetable||1|*|timetable([times], "name", [values], ...): a table with a time for every row (the times are numbers: days as datetime counts them, or any other measure); also timetable([times], [values], ..., "VariableNames", [names]) and "RowTimes", [times] at the end
istimetable||1|1|istimetable(x): whether x is a table with times
height||1|1|height(table): the number of its rows; of a matrix too
width||1|1|width(table): the number of its variables; the columns of a matrix
head||1|2|head(table[, k]): its first k rows (8 without k); the first entries of a list
tail||1|2|tail(table[, k]): its last k rows (8 without k); the last entries of a list
varnames||1|1|varnames(table): the names of its variables (fieldnames gives them too; getfield(table, name) is a variable as a list, setfield(table, name, [values]) sets one)
takerows||2|2|takerows(table, [numbers of rows]) or takerows(table, [truths]): the table of those rows; of values made by categorical too
takevars||2|2|takevars(table, [names]): the table of those variables, in that order
addvars||3|*|addvars(table, "name", [values]): one variable more; addvars(table, [values], ..., "NewVariableNames", [names][, "Before" | "After", name])
removevars||2|2|removevars(table, [names]): the table without those variables
renamevars||3|3|renamevars(table, [old names], [new names])
movevars||4|4|movevars(table, [names], "Before" | "After", name): those variables at another place
topkrows||2|4|topkrows(table, k[, names][, "ascend" | "descend"]): the k rows with the largest values of the named variables (the smallest with "ascend")
table2array|table2cell|1|1|table2array(table): its rows as a matrix
array2table|cell2table|1|3|array2table(matrix[, "VariableNames", [names]]): a table with a variable for every column
struct2table||1|1|struct2table(struct of lists) or struct2table([struct, ...]): a table with a variable for every field
table2struct||1|3|table2struct(table): a list with a struct for every row; table2struct(table, "ToScalar", true): one struct of lists
timetable2table||1|1|timetable2table(timetable): the table with the times as its first variable
table2timetable||1|3|table2timetable(table): the first variable becomes the times; table2timetable(table, "RowTimes", name or [times])
innerjoin||2|4|innerjoin(left, right[, "Keys", [names]]): the rows of both tables side by side where the keys (the variables that both have) agree, in rising order of the keys; variables of one name get _left and _right
outerjoin||2|6|outerjoin(left, right[, "Keys", [names]][, "Type", "left" | "right" | "full"]): as innerjoin, with the rows that have no partner too (what is not there is missing; the keys are merged)
varfun||2|2|varfun(f, table): the table of f applied to every variable (a list of values goes in, a value or a list comes out)
rowfun||2|2|rowfun(f, table): a table with the values of f for every row, the variables being its arguments
groupsummary||2|4|groupsummary(table, [group names][, method or [methods]][, [names]]): a row for every group with GroupCount and method_name for the variables of numbers; methods: sum mean median mode min max std var range numunique nummissing
groupcounts||2|2|groupcounts(table, [group names]): a row for every group with GroupCount and Percent
summary||1|1|summary(table): a table with Variable, Type, Min, Median, Max, NumMissing for every variable; of values made by categorical: Category and Count
stack||2|2|stack(table, [names]): those variables one under another: a row for every value, with name1_name2_Indicator saying which variable it was
unstack||3|3|unstack(table, value name, indicator name): a variable for every value of the indicator (several values of one group and indicator add up)
readtable||1|1|readtable("file.csv"): the table of a file with names in the first line (Var1, Var2, ... when that line holds numbers); numbers stay exact, other entries are texts, empty ones are missing
writetable||2|2|writetable(table, "file.csv"): the names, then a line for every row, commas between the entries
isregular||1|1|isregular(timetable or [times]): whether the times are one step apart
lag||1|2|lag(timetable[, k]): the values k rows later (1 without k), the times staying; what moves in from outside is missing
retime||2|5|retime(timetable, [new times][, method]): the table at other times; methods: fillwithmissing (as given), previous, next, nearest, linear, spline, pchip, or gathered over the stretch from a time to the next: mean sum min max median count firstvalue lastvalue; retime(timetable, "regular", method, "TimeStep", step)
synchronize||2|4|synchronize(timetable, timetable[, "union" | "intersection" | "first" | "last"][, method]): both tables at the times of both (or of one), with the methods of retime
categorical||1|5|categorical([values][, [kinds][, [names of the kinds]]][, "ordinal"]): values of a few kinds (those that differ, in rising order, when no kinds are given; a value of no kind is undefined)
iscategorical||1|1|iscategorical(x): whether x was made by categorical
categories||1|1|categories(c): the kinds of values made by categorical, in their order
countcats||1|1|countcats(c): how many values are of every kind (histcounts(c) too)
isundefined||1|1|isundefined(c): a truth for every value: whether it is of no kind
isordinal||1|1|isordinal(c): whether the kinds are in an order that means something
iscategory||2|2|iscategory(c, kind or [kinds]): whether c has that kind
addcats||2|2|addcats(c, [kinds]): more kinds at the end
removecats||1|2|removecats(c, [kinds]): without those kinds (their values become undefined); removecats(c): without the kinds that no value has
renamecats||2|3|renamecats(c, [new names]) or renamecats(c, [old], [new])
reordercats||1|2|reordercats(c, [kinds in a new order]); reordercats(c): in rising order
mergecats||2|3|mergecats(c, [kinds][, new name]): those kinds become one (the first, or the new name)
setcats||2|2|setcats(c, [kinds]): exactly those kinds; values of other kinds become undefined
grp2idx||1|1|grp2idx([values] or c): [numbers of the groups from 1, the groups]
cellstr||1|1|cellstr(c): the values made by categorical as a list of their kinds (double(c) gives the numbers of the kinds)
# more charts of data
bar3||1|*|bar3(matrix of heights): a bar of space for every entry, a row of the matrix along y
histogram2||2|*|histogram2([x values], [y values][, cells or [cells of x, cells of y]]): how many pairs fall into every cell of a grid, as bars of space (ten cells a side without a number)
surfc|meshc|1|*|surfc(matrix of heights) or surfc([x values], [y values], matrix): the surface of surf with its lines of equal height on the floor below it
streamline||7|*|streamline([u(x, y), v(x, y)], x, a, b, y, c, d[, [[x, y], ...]]): the lines that follow a field from the points given (from a grid of 36 points without them), each from the first colour of the palette to the last
polarhistogram|rose|1|*|polarhistogram([angles in radians][, parts]): how many angles fall into every part of the circle (12 parts without a number), as pieces that reach as far as their counts
dendrogram||1|*|dendrogram(tree): the tree of a clustering from the rows [group, group, height] that linkage gives; under every leaf its number
bubblechart||3|*|bubblechart([x values], [y values], [sizes]): dots whose widths show a third value
fimplicit3||10|*|fimplicit3(f(x, y, z), x, a, b, y, c, d, z, e, g): the surface where a function of three variables is zero (an equation will do), from a grid of space
isosurface||2|*|isosurface([matrix, matrix, ...], level): the surface where the numbers of a grid of space (a matrix for every z) pass a level
triplot||2|*|triplot(triangles, [x values], [y values]) or triplot(triangles, [[x, y], ...]): the sides of triangles given as rows of three numbers of points, as delaunay gives them
trisurf|trimesh|4|*|trisurf(triangles, [x values], [y values], [z values]): a surface of triangles of space
pareto||1|*|pareto([values]): the values as bars from the largest, and their running sum
fill|patch|1|*|fill([x values], [y values]) or fill([[x, y], ...]): a shape with those corners, filled; its area is told
ribbon||1|*|ribbon(matrix): the columns of a matrix as strips of space, one beside the other
normplot|qqplot probplot|1|*|normplot([values]): the sorted values against the places that a standard normal law gives their ranks, with the line through the quarters; values of a normal law lie along it
parallelplot||1|*|parallelplot(matrix): every row as a line across an axis for every column, each axis from its least value to its largest
swarmchart||1|*|swarmchart([values]) or swarmchart([[values], [values], ...]): the values of every group as dots that step aside for one another
violinplot||1|*|violinplot([values]) or violinplot([[values], ...]): the smooth density of every group to both sides of its place, with the median
histfit||1|*|histfit([values][, bins]): the counts of the values in bins and the normal law of their mean and spread
gscatter||3|*|gscatter([x values], [y values], [groups]): dots in the colour of their group
andrewsplot||1|*|andrewsplot(matrix): every row as the curve x1/sqrt(2) + x2*sin(t) + x3*cos(t) + x4*sin(2t) + ... from -pi to pi
+boxplot|boxchart
+surf|surfl
bar3h||1|*|bar3h(matrix of lengths): the bars of bar3 lying along x
cdfplot||1|*|cdfplot([values]): the share of the values at or below every value, in steps
plotmatrix||1|*|plotmatrix(matrix): every column against every other as dots, a chart for each pair; in its own cell the counts of a column
stackedplot||1|*|stackedplot(matrix): the columns as lines, each in a chart of its own, one above the other
biplot||1|*|biplot(matrix): an arrow from the origin for every variable by its weights in two directions, as the first two columns of pca give them
silhouette||2|3|silhouette(points, [groups][, "euclidean" | "sqeuclidean"]): for every point how well it sits in its group: (b - a)/max(a, b) with a the mean distance to its own group and b to the nearest other group (squared distances unless told otherwise; a point alone in its group gets 0)
# expressions as code, their shared parts, and as a picture of text
ccode||1|2|ccode(expression[, "name"]): the expression as a line of C that sets t0 (or the name): numbers as doubles, x*x for a square, pow, sqrt, exp, log, fabs, M_PI; a list or a matrix gives a line for every entry (t0[0], t0[1][2])
fortran||1|2|fortran(expression[, "name"]): the expression as a line of Fortran: 2.0D0, x**2, sqrt, exp, log; a list or a matrix gives a line for every entry (t0(1), t0(2,3))
matlabcode|octavecode|1|1|matlabcode(expression): the expression as code of MATLAB and Octave that works on arrays entry by entry (.* ./ .^); a matrix as [a, b; c, d]
matlabfunction||1|3|matlabfunction(expression[, "Vars", [x, y]]): the function @(x, y) expression of its variables (in the order of the alphabet unless they are given), to call or to hand to fzero, integral, ode45 and the like
subexpr||1|2|subexpr(expression[, name]): [the expression with sigma (or the name) for the part that it has more than once and that saves most, sigma = that part]
cse||1|1|cse(expression or [expressions]): [[sigma1 = part, sigma2 = part, ...], what is left]: every part that comes more than once named once, those that others use first
pretty||1|1|pretty(expression): the expression as text in several lines, as on paper: fractions over a bar, powers raised, brackets as tall as what they hold
# systems of control: one input, one output, as a ratio of polynomials in s (running time) or z (sampled)
!series|2|4|series(f, x, a, n): power series around a up to order n; series(G, H): two systems one after the other, G*H
!pade|2|5|pade(f, x, m, n): the ratio of polynomials of degrees m and n that agrees with f at 0 as far as possible; pade(f, x, x0, m, n) at x0; pade(T, n): [numerator, denominator] for the delay e^(-T*s) as padecoef
tf||1|3|tf([numerator], [denominator][, Ts]): the system with that transfer function, the highest power first; Ts the time between samples of a sampled system (-1: not said); tf("s"), tf("z", Ts): the variable itself; tf(system or a ratio of polynomials in s or z): written as tf
zpk||1|4|zpk([zeros], [poles], gain[, Ts]): the system by its zeros and poles; zpk(system): a system written that way
ss||1|5|ss(A, B, C, D[, Ts]): the system x' = A*x + B*u, y = C*x + D*u (sampled with Ts: x at the next sample); ss(system): a system written that way (the companion form of tf2ss)
tfdata||1|1|tfdata(system): [numerator, denominator], the highest power first
zpkdata||1|1|zpkdata(system): [zeros, poles, gain]
ssdata||1|1|ssdata(system): [A, B, C, D]
tf2sym||1|1|tf2sym(system): its transfer function as an expression in s, or z for a sampled system
tf2ss||1|2|tf2ss([numerator], [denominator]): [A, B, C, D] in the companion form that has the coefficients in the first row of A
ss2tf||4|4|ss2tf(A, B, C, D): [numerator, denominator] of C*inv(s*I - A)*B + D
tf2zp|tf2zpk|1|2|tf2zp([numerator], [denominator]): [zeros, poles, gain]
zp2tf||1|3|zp2tf([zeros], [poles], gain): [numerator, denominator]
ss2zp||4|4|ss2zp(A, B, C, D): [zeros, poles, gain]
zp2ss||1|3|zp2ss([zeros], [poles], gain): [A, B, C, D]
isct||1|1|isct(system): whether its time runs (it is not sampled)
isdt||1|1|isdt(system): whether it is sampled
isproper||1|1|isproper(system): whether the numerator has no higher degree than the denominator
isstable||1|1|isstable(system): whether every pole is in the left half of the plane (inside the unit circle for a sampled system)
pole||1|1|pole(system): the zeros of its denominator
zero|tzero|1|1|zero(system): the zeros of its numerator
dcgain||1|1|dcgain(system): the gain at frequency 0: the transfer function at s = 0, or at z = 1 for a sampled system
damp||1|1|damp(system): [natural frequencies, damping ratios, poles], the slowest pole first
parallel||2|2|parallel(G, H): two systems side by side with their outputs added, G + H
feedback||2|3|feedback(G, H[, sign]): G with H from its output back to its input, G/(1 + G*H); with the sign +1 the fed back signal is added, G/(1 - G*H)
minreal||1|1|minreal(system): the same system without the zeros and poles that cancel
evalfr||2|2|evalfr(system, s): the transfer function at that complex place
freqresp||2|2|freqresp(system, w or [w]): the transfer function at the frequency w: at s = i*w, or at z = e^(i*w*Ts) for a sampled system
bode|nichols|1|2|bode(system, [w]): [gains, phases in degrees] at those frequencies, the phase without jumps of 360; bode(system): [gains, phases, frequencies] at frequencies that it chooses
nyquist||1|2|nyquist(system, [w]): [real parts, imaginary parts] of the response at those frequencies; without them [real, imaginary, frequencies]
margin||1|1|margin(system): [gain margin, phase margin in degrees, frequency where the phase crosses -180, frequency where the gain crosses 1] of the loop; inf where nothing crosses
allmargin||1|1|allmargin(system): the record of every gain margin and phase margin with their frequencies, the delay margins, and whether the closed loop is stable
hinfnorm|getpeakgain|1|1|hinfnorm(system): [the largest gain over all frequencies, the frequency where it is]
h2norm||1|1|h2norm(system): the root of the energy of its impulse response; inf for a system that is not stable or not strictly proper
step||1|2|step(system): its answer to a step of height 1 as an expression in t (for a sampled system the first samples); step(system, t or [t]): the values at those times
impulse||1|2|impulse(system): its answer to an impulse as an expression in t (for a sampled system the first samples); impulse(system, t or [t]): the values at those times
lsim||2|3|lsim(system, u(t)): its answer to the input u, an expression in t; lsim(system, u(t), t or [t]): the values; lsim(sampled system, [samples of the input]): the samples of the output
stepinfo||1|1|stepinfo(system): the record of the step response: RiseTime (10% to 90%), SettlingTime (within 2%), SettlingMin, SettlingMax, Overshoot and Undershoot in percent, Peak, PeakTime
c2d||2|3|c2d(system, Ts[, "zoh" | "tustin" | "matched" | "impulse"]): the sampled system for the time Ts between samples; "zoh" (the usual) holds the input between samples
d2c||1|2|d2c(sampled system[, "zoh" | "tustin"]): the system of running time that c2d turns back into it
pid||1|4|pid(Kp, Ki, Kd, Tf): the controller Kp + Ki/s + Kd*s/(Tf*s + 1); what is left out is 0
rlocus||1|2|rlocus(system, k or [k]): the poles of the closed loop with the gain k, the roots of denominator + k*numerator; rlocus(system): [the poles for gains that it chooses, the gains]
esort||1|1|esort([poles]): by their real parts, the largest first
dsort||1|1|dsort([poles]): by their absolute values, the largest first
ctrb||2|2|ctrb(A, B): the matrix [B, A*B, A^2*B, ...]; of full rank when the input reaches every state
obsv||2|2|obsv(A, C): the matrix [C; C*A; C*A^2; ...]; of full rank when the output shows every state
lyap||2|3|lyap(A, Q): the X with A*X + X*A' + Q = 0; lyap(A, B, C): with A*X + X*B + C = 0
dlyap||2|2|dlyap(A, Q): the X with A*X*A' - X + Q = 0
gram||2|3|gram(system, "c" or "o"): the Gramian of steering or of watching; gram(A, B) = integral of e^(A*t)*B*B'*e^(A'*t), gram(A, C, "o") the other
care|icare|4|4|care(A, B, Q, R): [X, K, poles] with A'*X + X*A - X*B*inv(R)*B'*X + Q = 0, the gain K = inv(R)*B'*X and the eigenvalues of A - B*K
dare|idare|4|4|dare(A, B, Q, R): [X, K, poles] with A'*X*A - X - A'*X*B*inv(R + B'*X*B)*B'*X*A + Q = 0, the gain K and the eigenvalues of A - B*K
lqr||4|5|lqr(A, B, Q, R[, N]): [K, X, poles]: the feedback u = -K*x that makes the integral of x'*Q*x + u'*R*u + 2*x'*N*u least
dlqr||4|5|dlqr(A, B, Q, R[, N]): [K, X, poles]: the same for a sampled system and the sum over all samples
lqe||5|5|lqe(A, G, C, Q, R): [L, P, poles]: the gain of the watcher (Kalman's filter) for x' = A*x + G*w, y = C*x + v with the spreads Q of w and R of v
acker||3|3|acker(A, B, [poles]): the gain K that puts the eigenvalues of A - B*K at those places, for one input (the formula of Ackermann)
place||3|3|place(A, B, [poles]): the gain K that puts the eigenvalues of A - B*K at those places
hsvd||1|1|hsvd(system): the singular values of Hankel of a stable system, the largest first: how much every state of its balanced form carries from the input to the output
balreal||1|1|balreal(system): [the balanced system as ss(A, B, C, D), the singular values of Hankel]: its Gramians of steering and of watching are equal and diagonal; states that the input does not reach or the output does not show are taken out first
balred||2|3|balred(system, order[, "matchdc" | "truncate"]): the system with that many states that stays nearest: the weakest states of the balanced form are put at rest, so that the gain at frequency 0 stays ("matchdc", the usual), or cut off ("truncate")
covar||2|2|covar(system, W): [the spread of the output, the covariance of the states] when white noise of the intensity W drives the system
dlqe||5|5|dlqe(A, G, C, Q, R): [M, P, Z, poles]: the gain M of Kalman's filter for the sampled system x[n+1] = A*x[n] + G*w[n], y = C*x + v, the covariance P of the error before a sample is seen and Z after it
lyapchol||2|2|lyapchol(A, B): the upper triangle R with R'*R = X, where A*X + X*A' + B*B' = 0
dlyapchol||2|2|dlyapchol(A, B): the upper triangle R with R'*R = X, where A*X*A' - X + B*B' = 0
initial||2|3|initial(system, x0): the output of the system left alone from the state x0, an expression in t (the first samples of a sampled system); initial(system, x0, t or [t]): its values at those times
gensig||2|4|gensig("sine" | "square" | "pulse", period[, duration[, step]]): [values, times] of that signal; 5 periods and 64 steps a period unless said
lqi||3|6|lqi(system, Q, R) or lqi(A, B, C, D, Q, R): [K, X, poles]: the feedback u = -K*[x; xi] with the integral xi of the error r - y as a state more, so that the output follows r
ss2ss||2|2|ss2ss(system, T): the same system with the states T*x: ss(T*A*inv(T), T*B, C*inv(T), D)
ord2||2|2|ord2(wn, zeta): the system 1/(s^2 + 2*zeta*wn*s + wn^2) of the second order with the natural frequency wn and the damping zeta
d2d||2|2|d2d(sampled system, Ts): the same system sampled with another time Ts between samples (the input held between them)
reg||3|3|reg(system, K, L): the controller made of the gain K of the states and the gain L of the watcher, ss(A - B*K - L*C + L*D*K, L, -K, 0); it is fed back with the sign +1
filt||1|3|filt([numerator], [denominator][, Ts]): the sampled system with polynomials in 1/z, as the filters of signals are written
!sigma|1|2|sigma(n): the sum of the divisors of n, sigma(n, k) of their k-th powers; sigma(system, [w]): the gains of a system at those frequencies (its one singular value), without them [gains, frequencies]
bodeplot||1|*|bodeplot(system[, [frequencies]]): the gain in decibels above and the phase in degrees below, along the frequency by powers of ten
nyquistplot||1|*|nyquistplot(system[, [frequencies]]): the response in the plane as the frequency grows, with its mirror for the frequencies below zero and a cross at -1
pzplot|pzmap|1|*|pzplot(system): a cross at every pole and a circle at every zero; with the unit circle for a sampled system
rlocusplot||1|*|rlocusplot(system[, [gains]]): where the poles of the closed loop go as the gain grows from 0: from the poles (crosses) to the zeros (circles) or away without end
stepplot||1|*|stepplot(system[, the last time]): the answer to a step along the time (for a sampled system: the number of samples)
impulseplot||1|*|impulseplot(system[, the last time]): the answer to an impulse along the time (for a sampled system: the number of samples)
initialplot||2|*|initialplot(system, x0[, the last time]): the output of the system left alone from the state x0 along the time (for a sampled system: the number of samples)
nicholsplot||1|*|nicholsplot(system[, [frequencies]]): the gain in decibels against the phase in degrees as the frequency grows, with a cross at -180 degrees and 0 dB
# systems of control with several inputs and outputs: ss(A, B, C, D) of any sizes, or a matrix whose entries are systems with one input and one output. The functions of systems take them: tf ss zpk minreal pole zero dcgain evalfr freqresp sigma step impulse lsim initial norm hinfnorm covar series parallel feedback inv c2d d2c reg, and + - * / ^ join them
!append|1|*|append(list, x): the list with x at its end; append(G, H, ...): systems side by side, the inputs and the outputs of all of them and nothing between them
connect||4|4|connect(system, Q, [inputs], [outputs]): the system with loops closed: a row [input, outputs that go into it (with a minus sign: taken off)] of Q for every input that is fed; what stays are the inputs and outputs that are named, by their numbers
lft||2|4|lft(P, K): the loop of K around the last outputs and inputs of P (as many as K takes and gives); lft(P, K, nu, ny): around the last nu inputs and ny outputs
subsystem||3|3|subsystem(system, [outputs], [inputs]): the part of a system from those inputs to those outputs, by their numbers
iosize||1|1|iosize(system): [outputs, inputs]; size(system) gives the same
order||1|1|order(system): the number of states of a system (of the fewest that a matrix of systems needs)
augstate||1|1|augstate(system): the system with its states as more outputs, ss(A, B, [C; I], [D; 0])
kalman||3|4|kalman(system, Qn, Rn[, Nn]): [the filter of Kalman as a system with the inputs [u; y] and the outputs [estimate of y; estimate of x], its gain L, the covariance P of the error]; the last inputs of the system are the noise w of the covariance Qn (as many as Qn has rows), Rn is the covariance of the noise of the outputs, Nn the one between both; a sampled system gives [filter, L, P, M, Z] with the gain M of the newest measurement and the covariance Z after it
lqg||3|3|lqg(system, QXU, QWV): the controller of least cost for noisy states and outputs, as a system from y to u (fed back with the sign +1): QXU weighs states and inputs together, QWV is the covariance of the noise of the states and of the outputs together
lqgreg||2|2|lqgreg(kest, K): the controller made of the filter that kalman gives and of the gain K of the states, from y to u (fed back with the sign +1)
estim||2|4|estim(system, L): the watcher with the gain L, from the outputs y to [estimate of y; estimate of x]; estim(system, L, [sensors], [known]): with the outputs that are measured and the inputs that are known, by their numbers: inputs [u; y]
lqry||3|4|lqry(system, Q, R[, N]): [K, X, poles] as lqr, with the weight Q on the outputs instead of the states
!ctrb|1|2|ctrb(A, B): the matrix [B, A*B, A^2*B, ...]; of full rank when the input reaches every state; ctrb(system)
!obsv|1|2|obsv(A, C): the matrix [C; C*A; C*A^2; ...]; of full rank when the output shows every state; obsv(system)

# delays inside systems of control: a system of running time with dead time is an expression in s with e^(-T*s) in it, as tf([1], [1, 1], "InputDelay", 2) gives it or as it is written: e^(-2s)/(s + 1). The functions of systems take it: step impulse lsim (exact, also for delays inside loops at given times), evalfr freqresp dcgain bode nyquist sigma margin allmargin, pole zero isstable (of the ratio of polynomials beside one delay), series parallel feedback, c2d (exact with the input held, also for a part of a sample), pade(system, n)
hasdelay||1|1|hasdelay(system): whether it has a delay e^(-T*s) in it
totaldelay||1|1|totaldelay(system): the delay T of a system that is a ratio of polynomials times e^(-T*s); 0 without a delay
absorbdelay||1|1|absorbdelay(sampled system): the system itself, its delays are poles at 0 already; a delay of running time is no ratio of polynomials (pade gives one that stands for it)
thiran||2|2|thiran(T, Ts): the sampled filter that passes every frequency with the gain 1 and delays by T, which need not be a whole number of samples (Thiran): its order is T/Ts rounded up
!tf|1|5|tf([numerator], [denominator][, Ts]): the system with that transfer function, the highest power first; Ts the time between samples of a sampled system (-1: not said); tf("s"), tf("z", Ts): the variable as a system, for s := tf("s"); tf(system); tf(expression in s or z[, Ts]); tf(..., "InputDelay", T) (also "OutputDelay", "IODelay", or delay = T): with the dead time T, e^(-T*s) times the system (for a sampled system T samples: poles at 0)
!zpk|1|6|
!ss|1|7|

# filters and spectra of signals: windows, filters of Butterworth, Chebyshev, Cauer and Bessel, filters by windows, responses, spectra, peaks, prediction
rectwin|boxcar|1|2|rectwin(n): the window of n ones
hann||1|2|hann(n): the window 0.5 - 0.5*cos(2*pi*k/(n - 1)), zero at both ends; hann(n, "periodic"): the first n values of the window of n + 1
hanning||1|2|hanning(n): the window of Hann without its zeros at the ends, 0.5 - 0.5*cos(2*pi*k/(n + 1)) for k = 1..n
hamming||1|2|hamming(n): the window 0.54 - 0.46*cos(2*pi*k/(n - 1)); hamming(n, "periodic")
blackman||1|2|blackman(n): the window 0.42 - 0.5*cos(2*pi*k/(n - 1)) + 0.08*cos(4*pi*k/(n - 1)); blackman(n, "periodic")
blackmanharris||1|2|blackmanharris(n): the window of four cosines whose side lobes are 92 dB down
nuttallwin||1|2|nuttallwin(n): Nuttall's window of four cosines
flattopwin||1|2|flattopwin(n): the window of five cosines with a flat top, for reading the heights of peaks of a spectrum
triang||1|2|triang(n): the triangle without zeros at its ends
bohmanwin||1|2|bohmanwin(n): Bohman's window (1 - |x|)*cos(pi*|x|) + sin(pi*|x|)/pi for x from -1 to 1
parzenwin||1|2|parzenwin(n): the window of de la Vallee Poussin, pieces of cubic curves
gausswin||1|2|gausswin(n, alpha): the bell e^(-(alpha*x)^2/2) for x from -1 to 1; alpha is 2.5 unless given
tukeywin||1|2|tukeywin(n, r): flat in the middle, a cosine on the share r of the length at the ends (r = 0.5 unless given; 0 a rectangle, 1 a Hann window)
kaiser||1|2|kaiser(n, beta): the window of Kaiser, I0(beta*sqrt(1 - x^2))/I0(beta) for x from -1 to 1; beta is 0.5 unless given
chebwin||1|2|chebwin(n, r): the window of Dolph and Chebyshev whose side lobes are all r dB down (100 unless given)
barthannwin||1|2|barthannwin(n): the window of Bartlett and Hann, 0.62 - 0.48*|x| + 0.38*cos(2*pi*x) for x from -1/2 to 1/2
buttap||1|1|buttap(n): [zeros, poles, gain] of Butterworth's lowpass of order n with the edge 1 rad/s
cheb1ap||2|2|cheb1ap(n, Rp): [zeros, poles, gain] of Chebyshev's lowpass with Rp dB of ripple in the passband, edge 1
cheb2ap||2|2|cheb2ap(n, Rs): [zeros, poles, gain] of Chebyshev's lowpass of the second kind, whose stopband begins at 1 and is Rs dB down
ellipap||3|3|ellipap(n, Rp, Rs): [zeros, poles, gain] of Cauer's (elliptic) lowpass: Rp dB of ripple up to 1, Rs dB down in the stopband
besselap||1|1|besselap(n): [zeros, poles, gain] of Bessel's lowpass, whose delay is as flat as it can be; the product of the poles is 1
butter||2|4|butter(n, Wn): [b, a] of Butterworth's sampled lowpass of order n, 3 dB down at Wn (a share of half the sampling rate); butter(n, [w1, w2]): a bandpass; with "high", "stop"; with "s" a filter of running time, Wn in rad/s
cheby1||3|5|cheby1(n, Rp, Wp): [b, a] of Chebyshev's filter with Rp dB of ripple in its passband, which ends at Wp; with "high", [w1, w2], "stop", "s" as butter
cheby2||3|5|cheby2(n, Rs, Ws): [b, a] of Chebyshev's filter of the second kind, flat in the passband and Rs dB down from Ws on; the other arguments as butter
ellip||4|6|ellip(n, Rp, Rs, Wp): [b, a] of Cauer's (elliptic) filter: Rp dB of ripple up to Wp, Rs dB down in the stopband; the other arguments as butter
besself||2|3|besself(n, Wo): [b, a] of Bessel's filter of running time with the frequency Wo in rad/s; with "high", [w1, w2], "stop"
buttord||4|5|buttord(Wp, Ws, Rp, Rs): [the least order, Wn] of a Butterworth filter that loses Rp dB at most up to Wp and Rs dB at least from Ws on; "s" for running time
cheb1ord||4|5|cheb1ord(Wp, Ws, Rp, Rs): [the least order, Wp] for cheby1
cheb2ord||4|5|cheb2ord(Wp, Ws, Rp, Rs): [the least order, Ws] for cheby2
ellipord||4|5|ellipord(Wp, Ws, Rp, Rs): [the least order, Wp] for ellip
lp2lp||2|3|lp2lp(b, a, Wo): [b, a] of the lowpass of edge 1 moved to the edge Wo
lp2hp||2|3|lp2hp(b, a, Wo): [b, a] of the highpass with the edge Wo made of a lowpass of edge 1
lp2bp||3|4|lp2bp(b, a, Wo, Bw): [b, a] of the bandpass around Wo of the width Bw made of a lowpass of edge 1
lp2bs||3|4|lp2bs(b, a, Wo, Bw): [b, a] of the bandstop around Wo of the width Bw made of a lowpass of edge 1
bilinear||2|3|bilinear(b, a, fs): [b, a] of the sampled filter for one of running time, by s = 2*fs*(z - 1)/(z + 1)
iirnotch||2|2|iirnotch(w0, bw): [b, a] of the filter of second order that takes out the frequency w0, 3 dB down over the width bw (shares of half the sampling rate)
iirpeak||2|2|iirpeak(w0, bw): [b, a] of the filter of second order that passes the frequency w0 alone
fir1||2|5|fir1(n, Wn): the n + 1 coefficients of a lowpass by a Hamming window; fir1(n, [w1, w2]): a bandpass; with "high", "stop", a window (a name or n + 1 values), "noscale"
kaiserord||3|4|kaiserord([band edges], [amplitudes], [deviations][, fs]): [order, Wn, beta, kind] for fir1 with a Kaiser window that keeps within the deviations
sgolay||2|2|sgolay(order, framelen): the matrix whose rows fit a polynomial of that order to framelen values and give its value at every place (Savitzky and Golay)
sgolayfilt||3|3|sgolayfilt(x, order, framelen): every value by the polynomial fitted to the framelen values around it
freqz||1|3|freqz(b, a, n): [H, w]: the response B(e^(-iw))/A(e^(-iw)) of a sampled filter at n frequencies from 0 up to pi (512 unless given); freqz(b, a, [w]): at those
freqs||2|3|freqs(b, a, [w]): the response B(iw)/A(iw) of a filter of running time
grpdelay||1|3|grpdelay(b, a, n): [delays, w]: the delay of the envelope in samples, the slope of the phase turned round
impz||1|3|impz(b, a, n): the first n values of the answer of a sampled filter to one 1 (20 unless given; of a filter without a: its coefficients)
stepz||1|3|stepz(b, a, n): the first n values of its answer to ones
filtfilt||2|3|filtfilt(b, a, x): x through the filter forwards and backwards: no shift of the phase, the squared gain; filtfilt(butter(2, 0.3), x): with the pair [b, a] as one value, as everywhere a filter is taken
sosfilt||2|2|sosfilt(sections, x): x through sections of second order [b0, b1, b2, a0, a1, a2], one after the other
sos2tf||1|2|sos2tf(sections[, gain]): [b, a] of the product of the sections
tf2sos||1|2|tf2sos(b, a): [sections, gain]: the filter as sections of second order, every pair of poles with its nearest zeros, the poles nearest to the unit circle last
zp2sos||1|3|zp2sos(zeros, poles, gain): [sections, gain]; zp2sos(buttap(4)): with the three as one value
isminphase||1|2|isminphase(b, a): whether every zero lies inside the unit circle or on it, and every pole inside
ismaxphase||1|2|ismaxphase(b, a): whether every zero lies outside the unit circle or on it, and every pole inside
islinphase||1|2|islinphase(b, a): whether the filter delays every frequency alike: no a, and b a mirror of itself
firtype||1|1|firtype(b): the kind 1 to 4 of a filter with linear phase: even or odd symmetry, odd or even length
polystab||1|1|polystab(a): the polynomial with every zero outside the unit circle turned to the inside
periodogram||1|4|periodogram(x, window, nfft, fs): [Pxx, f]: the power of x over the frequencies up to half the sampling rate, from one transform; without fs per rad/sample over 0..pi
pwelch||1|5|pwelch(x, window, noverlap, nfft, fs): [Pxx, f] by Welch: the mean of the periodograms of overlapping pieces (unless given: Hamming pieces of 2/9 of the data, half shared)
cpsd||2|6|cpsd(x, y, window, noverlap, nfft, fs): [Pxy, f]: the cross spectrum of two signals by Welch's pieces
mscohere||2|6|mscohere(x, y, window, noverlap, nfft, fs): [Cxy, f]: |Pxy|^2/(Pxx*Pyy), from 0 to 1 how well one signal follows the other at every frequency
tfestimate||2|6|tfestimate(x, y, window, noverlap, nfft, fs): [Txy, f]: the transfer from x to y, conj(Pxy)/Pxx
spectrogram||1|4|spectrogram(x, window, noverlap, nfft): the transforms of overlapping pieces, a row for every frequency up to half the sampling rate, a column for every piece
bandpower||1|1|bandpower(x): the mean of the squares of a signal
rssq||1|1|rssq(x): the root of the sum of the squares
peak2rms||1|1|peak2rms(x): the largest absolute value over the root of the mean of the squares
enbw||1|2|enbw(window[, fs]): the width of the rectangle that passes as much noise as the window, in bins of the transform (in Hz with fs)
mag2db|db|1|1|mag2db(x): 20*log10(|x|)
db2mag||1|1|db2mag(x): 10^(x/20)
pow2db||1|1|pow2db(x): 10*log10(x)
db2pow||1|1|db2pow(x): 10^(x/10)
rceps||1|1|rceps(x): the real cepstrum, the inverse transform of ln|fft(x)|
goertzel||2|2|goertzel(x, k or [k]): the entries k of fft(x), each by a sum of its own
czt||1|4|czt(x, m, w, a): the z-transform of x at the m points a*w^(-k) (unless given: the unit circle once, as fft)
dftmtx||1|1|dftmtx(n): the matrix whose product with a column is its fft
cconv||2|3|cconv(a, b, n): the convolution of two lists around a circle of n places
envelope||1|1|envelope(x): [upper, lower]: the mean plus and minus the size of the analytic signal of what is left
findpeaks||1|*|findpeaks(x): [values, places] of the entries above both neighbours; with "MinPeakHeight", h, "Threshold", t, "MinPeakProminence", p, "MinPeakDistance", d, "NPeaks", n, "SortStr", "descend"
medfilt1||1|2|medfilt1(x, n): every value replaced by the median of the n values around it (3 unless given; zeros beyond the ends)
upfirdn||2|4|upfirdn(x, h, p, q): p - 1 zeros after every value, the filter h, then every q-th value
decimate||2|3|decimate(x, r, n): every r-th value after a Chebyshev lowpass of order n (8 unless given) both ways
buffer||2|3|buffer(x, n, p): the matrix whose columns are frames of n values, each sharing p values with the one before
xcov||1|2|xcov(x, y): the correlation at every shift of two lists without their means
levinson||1|2|levinson(r, p): [a, e, k]: the predictor of order p for the autocorrelations r by the recursion of Levinson and Durbin, the power of its error, the reflections
aryule||2|2|aryule(x, p): [a, e, k]: the autoregressive model of order p by the equations of Yule and Walker
arburg||2|2|arburg(x, p): [a, e, k]: the autoregressive model of order p by Burg's method
lpc||2|2|lpc(x, p): [a, g]: the linear predictor of order p and the power of its error
poly2rc||1|1|poly2rc(a): the reflections of a predictor polynomial
rc2poly||1|1|rc2poly(k): the predictor polynomial of reflections
sawtooth||1|2|sawtooth(t, width): the sawtooth of period 2*pi between -1 and 1; the top at the share width of the period (1 unless given; 1/2 gives a triangle)
rectpuls||1|2|rectpuls(t, w): 1 for t from -w/2 up to w/2, else 0 (w = 1 unless given)
tripuls||1|3|tripuls(t, w, s): the triangle of width w and height 1 around 0; s from -1 to 1 moves its top
gauspuls||3|3|gauspuls(t, fc, bw): a cosine of the frequency fc under a bell, with the share bw of fc as its band
chirp||4|5|chirp(t, f0, t1, f1[, "linear" | "quadratic" | "logarithmic"]): a cosine whose frequency goes from f0 at 0 to f1 at t1
diric||2|2|diric(x, n): Dirichlet's kernel sin(n*x/2)/(n*sin(x/2))
!hilbert|1|1|hilbert(n): the Hilbert matrix; hilbert([x]): the analytic signal of a list, whose imaginary part is its Hilbert transform
zplane||1|*|zplane(b, a): the zeros (circles) and the poles (crosses) of a sampled filter, with the unit circle
# more of the design of filters: least squares, samples of the response, equal ripples, another rate, lattices, phases, delays
firls||3|5|firls(n, f, a[, w]): the filter of the order n (n + 1 taps that read the same from both ends) with the least square error against the gains a at the edges f of the bands (pairs of frequencies from 0 to 1, 1 is half the sampling rate), a weight w for every band; an odd order with a gain at 1 takes one more; firls(n, f, a[, w], "hilbert"): taps of opposite signs from the two ends, the response -j times the gains (the transformer of Hilbert for the gain 1); firls(n, f, a[, w], "differentiator"): +j times the gains (the slope of a signal for the gains pi*f), the errors weighed by 1/f^2 where a gain is wanted
firpm|remez|3|5|firpm(n, f, a[, w]): the filter of Parks and McClellan: the same bands as firls, the greatest weighted error as small as it can be (ripples of one size), by the exchange of Remez with the greatest errors sought on the whole bands; firpm(n, f, a[, w], "hilbert") and firpm(n, f, a[, w], "differentiator") as with firls, the errors of a differentiator weighed by 1/f
fir2||3|6|fir2(n, f, m[, points of the grid[, points of a slope]][, window]): the filter whose response goes through the gains m at the frequencies f (from 0 to 1; two equal ones are a jump): the response on a grid, back to taps, times a window (Hamming unless given)
resample||3|5|resample(x, p, q): the values at p/q times the rate: p - 1 zeros after every value, the lowpass that MATLAB designs for it (least squares with Kaiser's window: a sinc that reaches 10 values of the slower signal to each side, shape 5), every q-th value; resample(x, p, q, n[, beta]): another reach and shape (n = 0 repeats the values); resample(x, p, q, b): with the taps b as the lowpass
phasez||1|3|phasez(b, a, n): [phases, w]: the phase of the response of a sampled filter at n frequencies from 0 up to pi (512 unless given), without jumps of 2 pi
phasedelay||1|3|phasedelay(b, a, n): [delays, w]: the phase turned round, over the frequency: the delay of a wave in samples
zerophase||1|3|zerophase(b, 1, n): [values, w]: the real response of a filter of linear phase (taps that read the same from both ends, or the same with the other sign) without the delay of half its length
finddelay||2|2|finddelay(x, y): by how many places y comes after x: the shift at which their correlation is greatest (of equal ones the nearest to 0)
dtw||2|3|dtw(x, y): [distance, places of x, places of y]: the two lists laid along each other with places that may stand again, so that the sum of the distances of the pairs is least; dtw(x, y, "squared") with squares
residuez||2|2|residuez(b, a): [residues, poles, polynomial part] of the ratio b/a of polynomials in 1/z: sum of r/(1 - p/z) (the next power for a pole that stands again) and k[1] + k[2]/z + ...
tf2latc||1|2|tf2latc(b): the reflections of the lattice of a filter b; tf2latc(b, a): [reflections of a, ladder] of the filter b/a (tf2latc([1], a) for a filter of poles alone)
latc2tf||1|2|latc2tf(k): the filter of a lattice with the reflections k; latc2tf(k, "allpole"): [[1], a]; latc2tf(k, v): [b, a] of a lattice with the ladder v
latcfilt||2|3|latcfilt(k, x): values through the lattice with the reflections k; latcfilt(k, v, x): through a lattice with a ladder; latcfilt(k, [1], x): poles alone
fftfilt||2|2|fftfilt(b, x): the values x through the filter with the taps b
filtic||3|4|filtic(b, a, y[, x]): the state of the filter b/a that has had the outputs y and the inputs x before (the latest first), as lfilter takes it
# a function of a complex number as a shape of four dimensions: frames are the third, colours the fourth
plot4d||1|*|plot4d(f, z, a, b): the function f of the complex number z in the box with the corners a and b as a shape of four dimensions: the real and the imaginary part of z and of f(z) lie on the width, the height, the frames and the colours of a picture (arg_real = width, arg_imag = height, val_real = frame, val_imag = color unless chosen otherwise: any of the four on any side); save_jxl("name.jxl", plot4d(sin(z))) saves the frames, whose alpha says where the shape is; plot4d(f, z, r) is from -r - r*i to r + r*i, plot4d(f, z) from -2 - 2i to 2 + 2i, the name z may be left out, f may be a text; chosen with name = value: width, height, frames, colors (512 each), palette (a name or colours), range_real = [least, greatest], range_imag, fill = surface | below | above | between (between: over every place of z the voxels from the real part of f(z) to its imaginary part, both on the side that z leaves, in the colours from the one to the other), thickness, rotate = [yaw, pitch, roll] or rotate(yaw, pitch, roll) (degrees that the body is turned), bits, effort, quality, distance, fps (16), and the words gray, reverse and normalize (every frame stretched on its own: what it holds fills the picture and takes the whole palette); as a statement: the frame in the middle as characters
view4d|plot4dview look4d snapshot4d|1|*|view4d(f, z, a, b): the shape of plot4d seen from outside as one picture, as an eye in space sees its box of voxels: what is hidden is left out, the surface is lighter where it faces the light, the frames of plot4d stand upright; with the usual sides the real part of f(z) stands as heights over the plane of z in the colours of the imaginary part. save_png("name.png", view4d(sin(z))) saves it (frames = n: the eye goes around). The eye is found by the program unless azimuth, elevation or viewpoint say where it stands (roll, zoom, perspective, fog, views, stereo as for the drawings of space); voxels = n or [across, upwards, frames] sets the cubes of the body, and every choice of the shape of plot4d is taken: arg_real, arg_imag, val_real, val_imag, fill, thickness, range_real, range_imag, rotate, normalize, colors, palette; as a statement it is a picture of characters
# quick looks: the commands that save, with small pictures that are packed fast
save_png_fast|savepngfast|1|*|save_png_fast("name.png", drawing, choices): save_png for a quick look: 256 pixels wide unless a width is chosen (plot4d: 256 x 256 pixels, 256 frames, 256 colours) and packed fast (level = 1); every choice of save_png may be given
save_jxl_fast|savejxlfast|1|*|save_jxl_fast("name.jxl", drawing, choices): save_jxl for a quick look: 256 pixels wide (plot4d: 256 x 256 pixels, 256 frames, 256 colours), lossy at the distance 1 unless lossless, quality or distance is chosen (alpha stays exact), effort = 3; every choice of save_jxl may be given
save_fast|savefast|2|*|save_fast("name.png", drawing, choices) or save_fast("name.jxl", ...): save_png_fast or save_jxl_fast by the end of the name
# the earth: ways on a sphere and on an ellipsoid. Angles are degrees unless "radians" is said last; an ellipsoid is [half axis, eccentricity], the radius of a sphere or a name like "wgs84" (metres); without one, lengths are arcs of the sphere in degrees
!distance|2|8|distance(p, q): the distance of two points; distance(lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): the length of the shortest way between two places of the earth, as an arc of the sphere in degrees, or with an ellipsoid ([half axis, eccentricity], a radius, or a name like "wgs84") along its geodesic in the unit of the half axis; distance("rh", ...): along the line of one course
azimuth||4|8|azimuth(lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): the course at the first place of the shortest way to the second one, from north over east, from 0 to 360 without 360; azimuth("rh", ...): the one course that leads there; at a pole it is counted from the meridian of the longitude given
reckon||4|8|reckon(lat, lon, length, course[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): [lat, lon] of the place a length away along a course on the shortest way (a great circle, on an ellipsoid a geodesic); reckon("rh", ...): with the course kept
haversine||4|6|haversine(lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2[, radius][, "radians"]): the length of the great circle between two places on a sphere, in km on the earth of 6371 km unless a radius is given
geoddistance||4|6|geoddistance(lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2[, ellipsoid]): [length, course at the first place, course at the second place] of the geodesic between two places, on WGS84 in metres unless an ellipsoid is given
geodreckon||4|6|geodreckon(lat, lon, length, course[, ellipsoid]): [lat, lon, course there] a length along the geodesic that starts with a course, on WGS84 in metres unless an ellipsoid is given
geodarea||2|4|geodarea([latitudes], [longitudes][, ellipsoid]): [area, length of the sides] of the polygon whose sides are geodesics, on WGS84 in metres unless an ellipsoid is given; the area is that of the part which is not more than half of the surface
areaint||2|4|areaint([latitudes], [longitudes][, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): the area inside a polygon whose sides are shortest ways, as a share of the whole surface; with an ellipsoid in the square of its unit
areaquad||4|6|areaquad(lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): the area between two parallels and two meridians, as a share of the whole surface; with an ellipsoid in the square of its unit
departure||3|5|departure(lon1, lon2, lat[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): the length along the parallel of a latitude between two meridians, by the difference of the longitudes as they are given
antipode||2|3|antipode(lat, lon[, "radians"]): [lat, lon] of the place on the other side of the earth
meanm||2|4|meanm([latitudes], [longitudes][, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): [lat, lon] of the middle of places: the direction of the sum of their directions from the middle of the earth
track2||4|8|track2(lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2[, ellipsoid][, "radians"][, places]): [[latitudes], [longitudes]] of places in equal steps along the shortest way between two places, 100 unless given after "degrees" or "radians"; track2("rh", ...): along the line of one course
track1||3|8|track1(lat, lon, course[, length or [from, to]][, ellipsoid][, "radians"][, places]): [[latitudes], [longitudes]] along the way that starts with a course, once round the sphere unless a length is given; track1("rh", lat, lon, course, length): with the course kept
gcwaypts||4|5|gcwaypts(lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2[, legs]): [[latitudes], [longitudes]] of the ends of equal legs of the great circle between two places (10 legs unless given)
scircle1||3|8|scircle1(lat, lon, radius[, [course1, course2]][, ellipsoid][, "radians"][, places]): [[latitudes], [longitudes]] of the circle of places that are a radius away from a middle (100 places; an arc between two courses)
scircle2||4|9|scircle2(lat, lon, lat2, lon2[, [course1, course2]][, ellipsoid][, "radians"][, places]): the circle around the first place through the second one
legs||2|3|legs([latitudes], [longitudes][, "rh" | "gc"]): [[courses], [lengths in nautical miles]] of the legs between places, along lines of one course unless "gc" is said
gc2sc||3|4|gc2sc(lat, lon, course[, "radians"]): [lat, lon, 90]: the great circle through a place with a course as the circle of 90 degrees around its pole
gcxgc||6|7|gcxgc(lat1, lon1, course1, lat2, lon2, course2[, "radians"]): [[lat, lat], [lon, lon]]: the two places where two great circles meet
scxsc||6|7|scxsc(lat1, lon1, radius1, lat2, lon2, radius2[, "radians"]): [[lat, lat], [lon, lon]]: the two places where two circles on the sphere meet
gcxsc||6|7|gcxsc(lat, lon, course, lat0, lon0, radius[, "radians"]): [[lat, lat], [lon, lon]]: where a great circle meets the circle of a radius around a place
rhxrh||6|7|rhxrh(lat1, lon1, course1, lat2, lon2, course2[, "radians"]): [lat, lon] where two lines of one course meet
interpm||3|5|interpm([latitudes], [longitudes], step[, "gc" | "rh"][, "radians"]): [[latitudes], [longitudes]] with places put in between so that no step of latitude or longitude is larger than given; in between along straight lines of latitude and longitude, great circles ("gc") or lines of one course ("rh")
# the earth: places in space. geodetic = latitude, longitude and height above the ellipsoid (WGS84 in metres unless given); ecef = x, y, z from the middle of the earth; enu = east, north, up and ned = north, east, down at a place; aer = course, elevation and distance as seen from a place
geodetic2ecef||3|5|geodetic2ecef(lat, lon, height[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): [x, y, z] from the middle of the earth, on WGS84 in metres unless an ellipsoid is given
ecef2geodetic||3|5|ecef2geodetic(x, y, z[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): [lat, lon, height] above the ellipsoid
lla2ecef||1|2|lla2ecef([lat, lon, height][, ellipsoid]): [x, y, z]; rows of places give rows
ecef2lla||1|2|ecef2lla([x, y, z][, ellipsoid]): [lat, lon, height]; rows of places give rows
geodetic2enu||6|8|geodetic2enu(lat, lon, height, lat0, lon0, height0[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): [east, north, up] of a place as seen from the place lat0, lon0, height0
enu2geodetic||6|8|enu2geodetic(east, north, up, lat0, lon0, height0[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): [lat, lon, height]
geodetic2ned||6|8|geodetic2ned(lat, lon, height, lat0, lon0, height0[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): [north, east, down]
ned2geodetic||6|8|ned2geodetic(north, east, down, lat0, lon0, height0[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): [lat, lon, height]
geodetic2aer||6|8|geodetic2aer(lat, lon, height, lat0, lon0, height0[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): [course, elevation, distance] of a place as seen from another
aer2geodetic||6|8|aer2geodetic(course, elevation, distance, lat0, lon0, height0[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): [lat, lon, height]
ecef2enu||6|8|ecef2enu(x, y, z, lat0, lon0, height0[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): [east, north, up]
enu2ecef||6|8|enu2ecef(east, north, up, lat0, lon0, height0[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): [x, y, z]
ecef2ned||6|8|ecef2ned(x, y, z, lat0, lon0, height0[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): [north, east, down]
ned2ecef||6|8|ned2ecef(north, east, down, lat0, lon0, height0[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): [x, y, z]
ecef2aer||6|8|ecef2aer(x, y, z, lat0, lon0, height0[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): [course, elevation, distance]
aer2ecef||6|8|aer2ecef(course, elevation, distance, lat0, lon0, height0[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): [x, y, z]
enu2aer||3|4|enu2aer(east, north, up[, "radians"]): [course, elevation, distance]
aer2enu||3|4|aer2enu(course, elevation, distance[, "radians"]): [east, north, up]
ned2aer||3|4|ned2aer(north, east, down[, "radians"]): [course, elevation, distance]
aer2ned||3|4|aer2ned(course, elevation, distance[, "radians"]): [north, east, down]
ecef2enuv||5|6|ecef2enuv(u, v, w, lat0, lon0[, "radians"]): a step in space as [east, north, up] at a place (turned, not moved)
enu2ecefv||5|6|enu2ecefv(east, north, up, lat0, lon0[, "radians"]): a step east, north and up at a place as a step in space
ecef2nedv||5|6|ecef2nedv(u, v, w, lat0, lon0[, "radians"]): a step in space as [north, east, down] at a place
ned2ecefv||5|6|ned2ecefv(north, east, down, lat0, lon0[, "radians"]): a step north, east and down at a place as a step in space
ecefoffset||6|8|ecefoffset(lat1, lon1, height1, lat2, lon2, height2[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): [dx, dy, dz] in space from the first place to the second one
elevation||6|8|elevation(lat1, lon1, height1, lat2, lon2, height2[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): [elevation, distance, course] of the second place as seen from the first one, on GRS80 in metres unless an ellipsoid is given
# the earth: ellipsoids, their radii and latitudes of other kinds
referenceellipsoid||1|2|referenceellipsoid("name"[, "unit"]): [half axis, eccentricity] of an ellipsoid with a name, in metres or in the unit said: wgs84, grs80, grs67, wgs72, wgs66, wgs60, clarke66, clarke80, airy1830, airy1849, bessel, everest, international, hayford, krasovsky, iau65, iau68, pz90, earth (WGS84), unitsphere, sun, moon, mercury, venus, mars, jupiter, saturn, uranus, neptune, pluto
wgs84ellipsoid||0|1|wgs84ellipsoid(["unit"]): [half axis, eccentricity] of WGS84, in metres or in the unit said
referencesphere||1|2|referencesphere("name"[, "unit"]): the radius of a body that is taken as a sphere: earth (6371000 m), moon, sun, mercury, venus, mars, jupiter, saturn, uranus, neptune, pluto, unitsphere
meridianarc||2|4|meridianarc(lat1, lat2[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): the length of the meridian from one latitude to another, on WGS84 in metres unless an ellipsoid is given
meridianfwd||2|4|meridianfwd(lat, length[, ellipsoid][, "radians"]): the latitude a length to the north along the meridian
spheroid||1|2|spheroid(ellipsoid): the facts of an ellipsoid as rows [name, value]: the half axes, the inverse of the flattening, the eccentricity, the flattening, the third flattening, the mean radius, the area and the volume; spheroid(ellipsoid, "name"): one of them
axes2ecc||1|2|axes2ecc(a, b): the eccentricity sqrt(a^2 - b^2)/a of an ellipse with the half axes a and b
majaxis||1|2|majaxis(b, e): the larger half axis for the smaller one and the eccentricity
minaxis||1|2|minaxis(a, e): the smaller half axis for the larger one and the eccentricity
ecc2flat||1|1|ecc2flat(e): the flattening 1 - sqrt(1 - e^2) for an eccentricity
flat2ecc||1|1|flat2ecc(f): the eccentricity sqrt(2*f - f^2) for a flattening
ecc2n||1|1|ecc2n(e): the third flattening (a - b)/(a + b) for an eccentricity
n2ecc||1|1|n2ecc(n): the eccentricity for the third flattening
rcurve||2|4|rcurve("parallel" | "meridian" | "transverse", ellipsoid, latitude[, "radians"]): the radius of the parallel of a latitude, of the meridian there, or of the surface across the meridian
rsphere||2|7|rsphere(kind, ellipsoid): the radius of a sphere for an ellipsoid: "biaxial" (the mean of the half axes; "norm" last: their geometric mean), "triaxial" ((2a + b)/3), "eqavol" (the same volume), "authalic" (the same area), "rectifying" (the same length of the meridian), "curve" (the mean radius of the surface at a latitude, 45 degrees unless given); rsphere("euler", lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2, ellipsoid): the radius of the surface along the way between two places
convertlat||4|5|convertlat(ellipsoid, latitude, "from", "to"[, "radians"]): a latitude as one of another kind: geodetic, geocentric, parametric, authalic, conformal, rectifying, isometric (the height on Mercator's map, a number without a unit)
geocentriclatitude||2|3|geocentriclatitude(latitude, flattening[, "radians"]): the angle at the middle of the earth for a latitude
parametriclatitude||2|3|parametriclatitude(latitude, flattening[, "radians"]): the parametric (reduced) latitude
geodeticlatitudefromgeocentric||2|3|geodeticlatitudefromgeocentric(latitude, flattening[, "radians"]): the latitude for the angle at the middle of the earth
geodeticlatitudefromparametric||2|3|geodeticlatitudefromparametric(latitude, flattening[, "radians"]): the latitude for a parametric one
# pictures as matrices: filters, shapes, parts that hang together, edges, levels of gray, noise, measures, colours, moves (in doubles; shares from 0 to 1 or whole samples)
fspecial||1|3|fspecial("average", size), fspecial("gaussian", size, sigma), fspecial("laplacian", alpha), fspecial("log", size, sigma), fspecial("prewitt"), fspecial("sobel"), fspecial("unsharp", alpha): the weights of a filter, for imfilter
imgaussfilt||1|2|imgaussfilt(picture, sigma): a picture blurred by a bell of the width sigma (0.5 unless given), the border pixels going on beyond the border
imboxfilt||1|2|imboxfilt(picture, size): every pixel as the mean of the box of that odd size around it (3 unless given)
medfilt2||1|3|medfilt2(picture, [rows, columns]): every pixel as the median of the box around it (3 by 3 unless given), zeros beyond the border; "symmetric": the picture mirrored there
ordfilt2||3|3|ordfilt2(picture, order, shape): every pixel as the order-th smallest of the pixels under the ones of a shape
stdfilt||1|2|stdfilt(picture, shape): the deviation of the pixels under a shape (3 by 3 unless given) around every pixel
rangefilt||1|2|rangefilt(picture, shape): the largest less the smallest of the pixels under a shape (3 by 3 unless given)
padarray||2|4|padarray(picture, [rows, columns], value | "replicate" | "symmetric" | "circular", "both" | "pre" | "post"): a picture with a border of that many pixels: of a value (0), of its border pixels, mirrored, or going round
imsharpen||1|5|imsharpen(picture, "Radius", r, "Amount", a): a sharper picture: what a blur of the radius r (1) takes away, a times (0.8) added again
strel||2|3|strel("disk", r), strel("square", w), strel("rectangle", [rows, columns]), strel("diamond", r), strel("octagon", r), strel("line", length, degrees): a shape as a matrix of ones and zeros (the disk is the true one, all pixels within r)
imdilate||2|2|imdilate(picture, shape): every pixel as the largest under the shape: light parts grow
imerode||2|2|imerode(picture, shape): every pixel as the smallest under the shape: light parts shrink
imopen||2|2|imopen(picture, shape): eroded, then dilated: light parts smaller than the shape go away
imclose||2|2|imclose(picture, shape): dilated, then eroded: dark gaps smaller than the shape go away
imtophat||2|2|imtophat(picture, shape): the picture less its opening: the small light parts
imbothat||2|2|imbothat(picture, shape): the closing less the picture: the small dark parts
bwperim||1|2|bwperim(picture, 4 | 8): the pixels of the parts that touch the background (by their sides unless 8)
imfill||1|2|imfill(picture, "holes"): the background that does not reach the border filled
bwlabel||1|2|bwlabel(picture, 4 | 8): the parts that hang together (by their 8 neighbours unless 4) numbered from 1, as they are met going down the columns
bwconncomp||1|2|bwconncomp(picture, 4 | 8): the record of the parts: Connectivity, ImageSize, NumObjects, PixelIdxList (the numbers of the pixels of every part, counted down the columns)
regionprops||1|2|regionprops(picture or numbered parts): for every part the record of its Area, Centroid [x, y], BoundingBox [x, y, width, height], EquivDiameter and Extent
bwareaopen||2|3|bwareaopen(picture, pixels, 4 | 8): without the parts of fewer pixels
imclearborder||1|2|imclearborder(picture, 4 | 8): without the parts that touch the border
bweuler||1|2|bweuler(picture, 4 | 8): the parts less their holes
bwdist||1|1|bwdist(picture): for every pixel the distance to the nearest pixel that is not zero
edge||1|3|edge(picture, "sobel" | "prewitt" | "roberts" | "canny", threshold): 1 where a picture has an edge: where the slope is above the threshold (twice the root of the mean of its squares unless given) and the largest across its way; "canny": blurred, thinned, and weak edges kept where they hang on strong ones ([low, high] thresholds as shares of the largest slope)
imgradientxy||1|2|imgradientxy(picture, "sobel" | "prewitt" | "central" | "intermediate"): [the slopes across, the slopes down]
imgradient||1|2|imgradient(picture, method): [the sizes of the slopes, their directions in degrees against the clock from the right]
graythresh||1|1|graythresh(picture): the level of Otsu, a share from 0 to 1: the level that parts the grays into the two most unlike sides
otsuthresh||1|1|otsuthresh([counts]): the level of Otsu for the counts of levels of gray, a share of the way from the first to the last
stretchlim||1|2|stretchlim(picture, share): [low, high]: the levels below which and above which a hundredth of the pixels lie (or that share)
imadjust||1|4|imadjust(picture, [low, high] in, [low, high] out, gamma): the grays from low to high spread over the range that comes out (0 to 1), bent by gamma; without the levels: those of stretchlim
histeq||1|2|histeq(picture, levels): the grays moved so that the levels (64 unless given) are as equally full as they can be
imnoise||2|4|imnoise(picture, "gaussian", mean, variance), imnoise(picture, "salt & pepper", density), imnoise(picture, "speckle", variance): a picture with noise drawn from the stream of rng, down the columns
mean2||1|1|mean2(picture): the mean of all pixels
std2||1|1|std2(picture): the deviation of all pixels
corr2||2|2|corr2(A, B): how alike two pictures of one size are, from -1 to 1
immse||2|2|immse(A, B): the mean of the squares of the differences of two pictures
psnr||2|3|psnr(picture, reference, peak): the peak signal to the noise in decibels; the peak is 1 for shares, 255 or 65535 for samples
entropy||1|1|entropy(picture): the bits of information of a pixel, from the counts of 256 levels
imabsdiff||2|2|imabsdiff(A, B): the sizes of the differences of two pictures
rgb2hsv||1|1|rgb2hsv([R, G, B]): hue, saturation and value, shares from 0 to 1, of the red, green and blue of a picture
hsv2rgb||1|1|hsv2rgb([H, S, V]): red, green and blue of hue, saturation and value
rgb2lab||1|1|rgb2lab([R, G, B]): L*, a* and b* of the sRGB colours of a picture (white of D65)
lab2rgb||1|1|lab2rgb([L, a, b]): the sRGB colours of L*, a* and b*
rgb2ycbcr||1|1|rgb2ycbcr([R, G, B]): luma and the two colour differences as television counts them (shares: 16/255 is black, 235/255 white)
ycbcr2rgb||1|1|ycbcr2rgb([Y, Cb, Cr]): red, green and blue again
ind2rgb||2|2|ind2rgb(matrix of numbers of colours, [[r, g, b], ...]): the picture of three channels with the colours of a list
imtranslate||2|2|imtranslate(picture, [right, down]): a picture moved by that many pixels (parts of a pixel by straight lines), zeros where nothing comes from
impyramid||1|2|impyramid(picture, "reduce" | "expand"): half as large after a blur, or twice as large
integralimage||1|1|integralimage(picture): the sums of all pixels above and to the left of every corner (a row and a column of zeros first)
normxcorr2||2|2|normxcorr2(template, picture): how well a template fits at every place of a picture, from -1 to 1; the largest entry is where its last corner lies
xcorr2||1|2|xcorr2(A, B): the sums of the products of two pictures at every shift; xcorr2(A): of A with itself
dct2||1|1|dct2(picture): the transform of cosines of its columns and of its rows
idct2||1|1|idct2(matrix): the picture of a transform of cosines

# diagrams of blocks and their simulation over time (what Simulink does with drawn diagrams)
sim|simulate|2|*|sim([blocks], t or [t0, t1][, "step", h][, "points", n]): the signals of a diagram of blocks over time: a record of the times t (101 unless points says more) and of the values of every signal there. A block is ["signal", "kind", ...]: the name of the signal that it makes, its kind, then its numbers and the names of the signals that it takes, as ["e", "sum", "u", "-y"] or ["y", "tf", [1], [1, 1], "e"]; simblocks() lists the kinds. Sources: constant, step, ramp, sine, pulse, clock, signal; sums, gains, products, formulas (fcn), abs sign sqrt exp log sin cos tan tanh, saturation, deadzone, quantizer, switch, min, max, lookup, relay, ratelimiter, derivative; with states: integrator, tf, ss, pid, delay; sampled: unitdelay, zoh, dtf. In doubles, as Simulink: the states go by Runge and Kutta's steps of order four (2000 over the time unless step says another), sampled blocks at their own times, the jumps of sources are steps of their own; a loop without a state in it is solved at every step
simblocks||0|0|simblocks(): the kinds of blocks of sim, each with its form

# files of MATLAB's own format for values (MAT-files of level 5)
matread|loadmat load|1|2|matread("file.mat"): the variables of a MAT-file as a record (getfield(record, "name") is one of them); matread("file.mat", "name"): the value of one. Whole numbers come exact, other doubles by their shortest decimal form; a row is a list, a column rows of one entry, a matrix rows; logical values are truths, char arrays texts, cell arrays cell arrays, structs records, sparse matrices matrices; packed files (version 7) are unpacked. Files of the version 7.3 (HDF5) and objects of MATLAB in a file (string arrays, tables, datetimes) are not read
matinfo||1|1|matinfo("file.mat"): [name, [rows, columns], kind] of every variable of a MAT-file
matwrite|savemat|2|*|matwrite("file.mat", record) or matwrite("file.mat", "name", value, ...): writes the values as the variables of a MAT-file that MATLAB, Octave and SciPy read: numbers as doubles (complex ones too), truths as logical values, texts as char arrays, lists of numbers as rows, matrices as matrices, cell arrays and lists of unlike things as cell arrays, records as structs, lists of records with the same fields as struct arrays, formulas as their texts; "compress" as the last word packs the values

# units of the user's own
newunit|defineunit|2|2|newunit("name", quantity): a new unit for this session, made of units that are there, as newunit("fortnight", 14 day) or newunit("warp", 3 c): the unit commands then read the name as a unit
removeunit||1|1|removeunit("name"): a unit that newunit made is a plain name again

# the earth: units of lengths and angles (exact), angles as texts, zones
km2nm||1|1|km2nm(x): kilometres as nautical miles of 1852 m
km2sm||1|1|km2sm(x): kilometres as statute miles (survey miles of 6336000/3937 m)
nm2km||1|1|nm2km(x): nautical miles as kilometres
nm2sm||1|1|nm2sm(x): nautical miles as statute miles
sm2km||1|1|sm2km(x): statute miles as kilometres
sm2nm||1|1|sm2nm(x): statute miles as nautical miles
deg2km||1|2|deg2km(x[, radius or "body"]): degrees of arc as kilometres on the earth of 6371 km, or on a sphere with a radius in km or a name (moon, mars, ...)
km2deg||1|2|km2deg(x[, radius or "body"]): kilometres as degrees of arc
deg2nm||1|2|deg2nm(x[, radius or "body"]): degrees of arc as nautical miles (a radius in nautical miles)
nm2deg||1|2|nm2deg(x[, radius or "body"]): nautical miles as degrees of arc
deg2sm||1|2|deg2sm(x[, radius or "body"]): degrees of arc as statute miles (a radius in statute miles)
sm2deg||1|2|sm2deg(x[, radius or "body"]): statute miles as degrees of arc
rad2km||1|2|rad2km(x[, radius or "body"]): radians of arc as kilometres
km2rad||1|2|km2rad(x[, radius or "body"]): kilometres as radians of arc
rad2nm||1|2|rad2nm(x[, radius or "body"]): radians of arc as nautical miles
nm2rad||1|2|nm2rad(x[, radius or "body"]): nautical miles as radians of arc
rad2sm||1|2|rad2sm(x[, radius or "body"]): radians of arc as statute miles
sm2rad||1|2|sm2rad(x[, radius or "body"]): statute miles as radians of arc
unitsratio||2|2|unitsratio("to", "from"): the number that turns a length of one unit into another (m, km, cm, mm, micron, nm, ft, in, yd, mi, sm, sf), or an angle (degrees, radians): unitsratio("cm", "in") is 127/50
earthradius||0|1|earthradius(["unit"]): the mean radius of the earth, 6371000 m, in the unit said
distdim||3|4|distdim(x, "from", "to"[, radius or "body"]): a length or an arc in another unit; between a length and an arc with the radius of the sphere (the earth unless given, in the unit of the length)
angledim||3|3|angledim(x, "from", "to"): an angle in another unit: degrees, radians
todegrees||2|2|todegrees("unit", x): an angle of a unit in degrees
toradians||2|2|toradians("unit", x): an angle of a unit in radians
fromdegrees||2|2|fromdegrees("unit", x): degrees in a unit
fromradians||2|2|fromradians("unit", x): radians in a unit
wrapto180|npi2pi|1|1|wrapto180(x): degrees brought between -180 and 180 by whole turns
wrapto360|zero22pi|1|1|wrapto360(x): degrees brought between 0 and 360; a positive multiple of 360 gives 360
wraptopi||1|1|wraptopi(x): radians brought between -pi and pi by whole turns
wrapto2pi||1|1|wrapto2pi(x): radians brought between 0 and 2*pi; a positive multiple of 2*pi gives 2*pi
roundn||1|2|roundn(x, n): x rounded to the nearest multiple of 10^n (n = -2, hundredths, unless given)
dms2degrees||1|1|dms2degrees([degrees, minutes, seconds]): the degrees; the first part that is not 0 carries the sign; rows give a list
dm2degrees||1|1|dm2degrees([degrees, minutes]): the degrees
degrees2dms||1|1|degrees2dms(x): [degrees, minutes, seconds]; a list gives rows
degrees2dm||1|1|degrees2dm(x): [degrees, minutes]
angl2str||1|4|angl2str(x, "ns" | "ew" | "pm" | "none", "degrees" | "degrees2dm" | "degrees2dms" | "radians", n): an angle as a text, with N or S, E or W, a sign, or the sign only when negative; the last digit is at 10^n (n = -2 unless given)
str2angle||1|1|str2angle("12°30'15''S"): the degrees of a text with degrees, minutes and seconds, negative with S or W (also 12-30-15S, 12d30m15s, 123015S, -12.5)
timezone||1|1|timezone(lon): [hours, letter] of the zone of the sea at a longitude: the hours to add for the time of Greenwich, and its letter (Z at Greenwich, A to M to the east, N to Y to the west)
utmzone||1|2|utmzone(lat, lon): the zone of UTM of a place, like "31U"; utmzone("31U"): [[south, north], [west, east]] of a zone in degrees
# the earth: maps. projfwd("map", lat, lon, choices) gives [x, y], projinv("map", x, y, choices) gives [lat, lon]
projfwd|mfwdtran|3|*|projfwd("map", lat, lon[, choices]): [x, y] of a place on a map: mercator, webmercator, equirectangular (pcarree), sinusoidal, cylindricalequalarea (eqacylin), mollweide, stereographic, gnomonic, orthographic, azimuthalequidistant (eqdazim), azimuthalequalarea (eqaazim), transversemercator (tranmerc), utm, lambertconformal (lambert), albers (eqaconic), equidistantconic (eqdconic); choices: "origin", [lat, lon]; "parallels", [lat1, lat2]; "scale", k; "ellipsoid", e (the unit sphere unless given; utm and webmercator: WGS84); "zone", "31U"; "falseeasting", x; "falsenorthing", y; "radians"
projinv|minvtran|3|*|projinv("map", x, y[, choices]): [lat, lon] of a point of a map, with the choices of projfwd (utm needs "zone", "31U")
deg2utm||2|2|deg2utm(lat, lon): [x, y, zone] of a place in UTM on WGS84, in metres
utm2deg||3|3|utm2deg(x, y, "zone"): [lat, lon] of a point of a zone of UTM
# networks of neurons: the functions of their units (exact; of a name a formula that has a slope), for a number or every entry of a list
relu|poslin|1|1|relu(x): x where it is positive, else 0; of a name (x + abs(x))/2
leakyrelu||1|2|leakyrelu(x, a): x where it is positive, else a*x (a = 1/100 unless given)
clippedrelu||1|2|clippedrelu(x, c): 0 below 0, x up to c, then c (c = 6 unless given)
elu||1|2|elu(x, a): x where it is positive, else a*(e^x - 1) (a = 1 unless given)
gelu||1|1|gelu(x): x/2*(1 + erf(x/sqrt(2))): x times the chance that a normal number is below x
swish|silu|1|1|swish(x): x/(1 + e^(-x))
mish||1|1|mish(x): x*tanh(ln(1 + e^x))
softplus||1|1|softplus(x): ln(1 + e^x)
softsign|elliotsig|1|1|softsign(x): x/(1 + abs(x))
sigmoid|logsig logistic|1|1|sigmoid(x): 1/(1 + e^(-x)), from 0 to 1
tansig||1|1|tansig(x): tanh(x), from -1 to 1
purelin||1|1|purelin(x): x itself
satlin||1|1|satlin(x): 0 below 0, x up to 1, then 1
satlins||1|1|satlins(x): -1 below -1, x up to 1, then 1
hardlim||1|1|hardlim(x): 1 from 0 on, else 0
hardlims||1|1|hardlims(x): 1 from 0 on, else -1
radbas||1|1|radbas(x): e^(-x^2)
tribas||1|1|tribas(x): 1 - abs(x) between -1 and 1, else 0
netinv||1|1|netinv(x): 1/x
softmax||1|1|softmax(list): e^x over the sum of all e^x: chances that add up to 1; of a matrix for every row
compet||1|1|compet(list): 1 at the largest entry (the first of them), 0 elsewhere; of a matrix for every row
# networks of neurons: losses (exact; rows are observations, a flat list holds one number of every observation)
mse||2|2|mse(y, t): the mean of the squares of y - t over all entries
sse||2|2|sse(y, t): the sum of the squares of y - t
mae||2|2|mae(y, t): the mean of abs(y - t) over all entries
l2loss||2|2|l2loss(y, t): the sum of the squares of y - t over the number of observations (rows)
l1loss||2|2|l1loss(y, t): the sum of abs(y - t) over the number of observations
huber||2|3|huber(y, t, d): the loss of Huber: (y - t)^2/2 up to the distance d, then d*(abs(y - t) - d/2), summed, over the number of observations (d = 1 unless given)
crossentropy||2|2|crossentropy(y, t): -sum(t*ln(y)) over the number of observations: the chances y of the classes against the targets t (a flat list is one observation)
# networks of neurons: the operations of layers
fullyconnect||2|3|fullyconnect(x, W, b): W*x + b for a list x; for rows of observations a row of answers each
dlconv||2|7|dlconv(x, w, bias, "stride", s, "padding", p): the window w moved over the list or matrix x, at every place the sum of the products (no turning round, as layers of networks do); "padding", "same" keeps the size
maxpool||2|6|maxpool(x, size, "stride", s, "padding", p): the largest value under a window of the size moved over a list or a matrix (size [down, across] or one number; stride 1 unless given)
avgpool||2|6|avgpool(x, size, "stride", s, "padding", p): the mean under a window moved over a list or a matrix
batchnorm||3|5|batchnorm(x, offset, scale): every column of the rows x brought to the mean 0 and the variance 1 (plus 1/100000 under the root), then times scale plus offset; batchnorm(x, offset, scale, mean, variance): with a mean and a variance that are given
layernorm||3|3|layernorm(x, offset, scale): every row brought to the mean 0 and the variance 1, then times scale plus offset
embed||2|2|embed(k, W): row k of the matrix W; for a list of numbers the rows
lstm||6|6|lstm(X, h0, c0, W, R, b): a layer with long and short memory over the rows X of a sequence: [the hidden values after every row, the last hidden values, the last cell values]; W is 4h by d, R is 4h by h, b has 4h entries, for the gates in, forget, new and out
gru||5|5|gru(X, h0, W, R, b): a layer of gated units over the rows X of a sequence: [the hidden values after every row, the last ones]; W is 3h by d, R is 3h by h, b has 3h entries, for the gates reset, update and new
onehotencode||1|2|onehotencode(labels, classes): for every label a row with 1 at its class and 0 elsewhere (the classes are the labels in rising order unless given)
onehotdecode||1|2|onehotdecode(scores, classes): for every row of scores the class of its largest one (the numbers 1, 2, ... unless classes are given)
ind2vec||1|2|ind2vec([indices], n): the matrix with a column for every index, 1 in the row of the index and 0 elsewhere
vec2ind||1|1|vec2ind(M): for every column the row of its largest entry
confusionmat||2|3|confusionmat(known, guessed, classes): the matrix that counts in row i and column j the observations of class i that were taken for class j
mapminmax||1|3|mapminmax(x, a, b): every row of x brought between a and b (-1 and 1 unless given) by its least and largest entry
mapstd||1|3|mapstd(x, m, s): every row of x brought to the mean m and the deviation s (0 and 1 unless given)
divideblock||1|4|divideblock(n, a, b, c): [train, check, test]: the numbers 1 to n cut into three blocks with the shares a, b and c (0.7, 0.15 and 0.15 unless given)
dlgradient||2|2|dlgradient(y, [names]): the slopes of y by the names; of a list of values a list for every name
sgdmupdate||3|5|sgdmupdate(w, slopes, v, rate, momentum): [w + v', v'] with v' = momentum*v - rate*slopes (rate 1/100, momentum 9/10 unless given; v = [] at first)
adamupdate||5|9|adamupdate(w, slopes, m, v, t, rate, b1, b2, eps): [w', m', v']: a step of Adam with the means m and v of the slopes and of their squares and the number t of the step (rate 1/1000, b1 = 9/10, b2 = 999/1000, eps = 1/10^8 unless given; m = v = [] at first)
rmspropupdate||3|6|rmspropupdate(w, slopes, v, rate, decay, eps): [w', v'] with v' = decay*v + (1 - decay)*slopes^2 and w' = w - rate*slopes/(sqrt(v') + eps) (rate 1/1000, decay 9/10, eps = 1/10^8 unless given)
# networks of neurons: a network is a list of layers [weights, biases, "function"], a row of the weights for every unit
feedforwardnet|fitnet|1|3|feedforwardnet([inputs, units, ..., outputs], "function", seed): a network of dense layers with small weights that are the same on every machine (another seed gives others): the inner layers apply the function ("tanh" unless given), the last one "purelin"
patternnet||1|3|patternnet([inputs, units, ..., classes], "function", seed): the same with "softmax" in the last layer: the chances of classes
predict|forward|2|2|predict(net, x): what a network gives for an observation x or for the rows of observations; exact with names and fractions where its functions keep them
classify||2|3|classify(net, x, classes): the class with the largest value that goes out, for every observation (the numbers 1, 2, ... unless classes are given)
perform||3|4|perform(net, x, t, "mse" | "sse" | "mae" | "crossentropy"): the loss of a network on observations with their targets (the entropy for a last layer of "softmax", else the mean of the squares)
trainnet|train|3|*|trainnet(net, x, t): the network with the weights and biases that make its loss on the observations x with the targets t least (the mean of the squares, or with a last layer of "softmax" the entropy, where t may hold the numbers of the classes); choices: "epochs", n (1000 steps at most), "l2", x (a part x/2 times the sum of the squares of the weights), "tolerance", x, "mse" | "crossentropy", "report" (gives [network, loss, steps, largest slope]); trainnet(net, x, t, "solver", "sgdm" | "adam" | "rmsprop", ...) or with what trainingoptions gives: training by steps down the slope of the loss on batches of observations ("epochs", n: 30; "batch", n: 128; "rate", x; "momentum", x; "l2", x: 1/10000; "shuffle", "once" | "never" | "every-epoch"; "seed", n; "report" gives [network, the loss after every epoch]); a network of layers of windows is always trained by steps (Adam unless said)
perceptron||2|3|perceptron(x, t, rounds): one layer of "hardlim" units taught by the rule of the perceptron: for every wrong answer the weights move by the observation (100 rounds at most); exact
newlind||2|2|newlind(x, t): one layer of "purelin" units with the weights of least squares; exact
newrbe||2|3|newrbe(x, t, spread): a network that goes through every target: a unit of radbas around every observation (half its height at the distance spread, 1 unless given) and a "purelin" layer

# solids of four dimensions: solid4d("name", numbers...). They can be turned in the six planes of 4D, scaled, moved and cut; plot4d, view4d and wire4d draw them
solid4d||1|*|solid4d("name", numbers...): a solid of four dimensions around the origin, of the size 1 (its radius, the radius of its balls and disks, the distance of its vertices from the middle; a height goes from -1 to 1 along w): "ball", "ellipsoid", a, b, c, d; the regular polychora "5-cell", "tesseract", "16-cell", "24-cell", "120-cell", "600-cell"; the uniform polychora: "rectified", "truncated", "bitruncated", "cantellated", "runcinated", "cantitruncated", "runcitruncated" or "omnitruncated" before one of them, as "truncated 120-cell", or a Coxeter diagram as "x5o3o3x" (x a ring, o none, 2 no branch: "x3o2x7o" is a duoprism), "dual" before it for the dual; "snub 24-cell", "grand antiprism"; the ten regular star polychora by name ("small stellated 120-cell", "great 120-cell", "grand 120-cell", "great stellated 120-cell", "grand stellated 120-cell", "great grand 120-cell", "icosahedral 120-cell", "great icosahedral 120-cell", "grand 600-cell", "great grand stellated 120-cell") or by Schlaefli symbol ("{5/2,5,3}"): all that the polychoron winds around; "prism", "pyramid" and "bipyramid" over a uniform polyhedron ("cube", "truncated icosahedron", "snub cube", "7-gonal prism", "pentagonal antiprism", a diagram "x5x3o"); "duoprism", p, q and "duopyramid", p, q of two regular polygons; "spherinder" (ball by segment), "cubinder" (disk by square), "duocylinder" (disk by disk), "prismic cylinder", sides (disk by polygon); "hypercone", "hyperfrustum", lower, upper (over a ball), "coninder" (cone by segment), "cylindrone" (a cone over a cylinder), "dicone"; "hypercapsule", half length; the tori "spheritorus", R, r (a tube around a circle), "torisphere", R, r (around a sphere), "tiger", R1, R2, r (around a flat torus), "ditorus", R, a, r (around a torus), "torinder", R, r (torus by segment); the walls around surfaces without an inside "Klein bottle", R, r, thickness, half-turns, "projective plane", radius, thickness, "Moebius band", R, half width, thickness, half-turns; "paraboloid", "hyperboloid", waist, "spherical segment", lower, upper, "lens", thickness; "reuleaux", "rotor" | "simplex" (bodies of one width); "star cell", cells, core, spike, sharpness (a ball with the spikes of a regular polychoron); "superquadric", power (the sum of the powers of the four sizes is 1 at most); "bracketope", "shape": round brackets are the root of the sum of the squares, square ones the largest, angle ones the sum, as "[(II)(II)]" for the duocylinder
rotate4d||3|3|rotate4d(solid, "xw", degrees): the solid turned in one of the planes "xy", "xz", "xw", "yz", "yw", "zw"
scale4d||2|2|scale4d(solid, s) or scale4d(solid, [sx, sy, sz, sw]): the solid that many times as large, or along every axis by its own factor
move4d|translate4d|2|2|move4d(solid, [x, y, z, w]): the solid moved by that much
section4d||2|2|section4d(solid, c): the solid cut by w = c: plot4d and view4d show the cut, a solid of three dimensions, with the colours that say how deep a place lies in the solid along w (turn the solid first to cut it another way)
inside4d||2|2|inside4d(solid, [x, y, z, w]): whether a point is in a solid; for a list of points a list
volume4d|bulk4d|1|1|volume4d(solid): the bulk of a solid (its content of four dimensions): exact where a formula gives it, from the sides of a polytope (twelve digits), else by counting along the lines of a grid (three digits)
surface4d||1|1|surface4d(solid): the surface of a solid (the content of three dimensions of its border): exact where a formula gives it, from the sides of a polytope (twelve digits); a solid that was scaled along one axis more than along another has none
vertices4d||1|1|vertices4d(solid): the vertices of a polytope (the regular, uniform and star polychora, their duals, a duoprism) as it stands, a list of points
edges4d||1|1|edges4d(solid): the edges of a polytope as pairs of the numbers of its vertices
density4d||2|2|density4d(solid, [x, y, z, w]): how many times a solid covers a point: 1 inside and 0 outside, and for a star polychoron how many times it winds around the point (4 near the middle of the small stellated 120-cell, 191 for the grand 600-cell); for a list of points a list
wire4d|wireframe4d|1|*|wire4d(solid[, "xw"[, "yz"]]): the lines of a solid of four dimensions as an eye outside of the fourth dimension sees them: the edges of a polytope, of other solids the borders of their cuts by planes of two axes; what lies farther along w is smaller, the colours go along w. A drawing of space: saved with frames = n the solid turns once in the plane that is named ("xw" unless given; two planes: in both at once)

# walks of chance. They are drawings as walk and walk3d are: save_png / save_jxl save them, with frames they grow or turn
randomwalk|randwalk|0|*|randomwalk(n[, directions | "any" | "normal" | "levy"][, seed]): n steps of chance in the plane (1000 unless given): each of the 4 directions as likely as another, or of that many directions; "any": the length 1 in any direction; "normal": the parts along the axes follow the normal law (the motion of Brown); "levy": any direction, lengths with a heavy tail. Without a seed the numbers of chance are those of the session: the same every time unless rng(...) began them otherwise
randomwalk3d|randwalk3d|0|*|randomwalk3d(n[, "lattice" | "any" | "normal" | "levy"][, seed]): n steps of chance in space: along the six directions of the axes unless said
!walk|1|*|walk(x, base, digits): a walk along the digits of a real number: the digit d is a step of length 1 at 360*d/base degrees; walk(pi, 10, 1000); base 10 and 1000 digits when they are left out; a number whose digits end gives a walk that ends; walk(random, base, digits[, seed]): along digits of chance
!walk3d|1|*|walk3d(z, base, digits): a walk in space along the digits of a complex number: the digits of the real part turn the step around the z-axis, the digits of the imaginary part lift it above the plane; walk3d(e + pi*i, 12, 10000); walk3d(random, base, digits[, seed]): along two rows of digits of chance

# networks of neurons: layers that slide windows over pictures, and training by steps on a few observations at a time. Such a network is a list of layers that begins with ["input", [height, width, channels]]; an observation is a matrix (a list of matrices for several channels), several observations are a list of them
imageinputlayer||1|3|imageinputlayer([height, width, channels]): the layer that says what pictures come in, ["input", [height, width, channels]] (one channel unless said)
featureinputlayer||1|1|featureinputlayer(n): the layer that says that lists of n values come in
convolution2dlayer||2|6|convolution2dlayer(size, filters[, "stride", s][, "padding", p | "same"]): a layer of windows of that size (a number or [down, across]): every filter gives a channel of the sums of the products of its window with what it lies on, plus a bias; dlnetwork gives it its weights
maxpooling2dlayer||1|3|maxpooling2dlayer(size[, "stride", s]): the largest value under a window moved over every channel (stride 1 unless given)
averagepooling2dlayer||1|3|averagepooling2dlayer(size[, "stride", s]): the mean under the window
flattenlayer||0|0|flattenlayer(): the channels as one list of values, for the dense layers that follow
fullyconnectedlayer||1|1|fullyconnectedlayer(units): a dense layer of that many units; dlnetwork gives it its weights
relulayer||0|0|relulayer(): the layer ["relu"]: every value below 0 becomes 0
tanhlayer||0|0|tanhlayer(): the layer ["tanh"]
sigmoidlayer||0|0|sigmoidlayer(): the layer ["sigmoid"]
softmaxlayer||0|0|softmaxlayer(): the layer ["softmax"]: the values as chances of classes
leakyrelulayer||0|1|leakyrelulayer(scale): the layer ["leakyrelu", scale]: values below 0 times the scale (1/100 unless given)
dlnetwork||1|2|dlnetwork([imageinputlayer(...), layers ...][, seed]): the network of those layers with the sizes worked out and first weights of Glorot's kind that are the same on every machine (another seed gives others): ["conv", weights (filters of channels of matrices), biases, stride, padding], ["dense", weights, biases], ...
trainingoptions||1|*|trainingoptions("sgdm" | "adam" | "rmsprop"[, "MaxEpochs", n][, "MiniBatchSize", n][, "InitialLearnRate", x][, "Momentum", x][, "L2Regularization", x][, "Shuffle", "once" | "never" | "every-epoch"][, "seed", n]): the choices of a training by steps, as a list that trainnet takes

# the earth: grids of values (raster maps). A grid is a matrix with a reference that says where its samples lie: cells that fill the limits, or postings on them
georefcells||3|8|georefcells([south, north], [west, east], [rows, columns]): the reference of a grid of cells of the earth, as a record (LatitudeLimits, LongitudeLimits, RasterSize, ...); georefcells(latitudes, longitudes, degrees of latitude of a cell, degrees of longitude): by the size of a cell; the first row is the southern one and the first column the western one unless ColumnsStartFrom = "north" or RowsStartFrom = "east" is said
georefpostings||3|8|georefpostings([south, north], [west, east], [rows, columns]): the reference of a grid of postings, whose outermost samples lie on the limits; or by the degrees between two samples
maprefcells||3|8|maprefcells([x from, to], [y from, to], [rows, columns]): the reference of a grid of cells of a plane (a map); or by the sizes of a cell in x and y
maprefpostings||3|8|maprefpostings([x from, to], [y from, to], [rows, columns]): the reference of a grid of postings of a plane; or by the ways between two samples
refvectogeorasterreference||2|2|refvectogeorasterreference([cells a degree, northern latitude, western longitude], [rows, columns]): the reference of a grid of the older kind (three numbers); those three numbers are taken wherever a reference is
geographictointrinsic||3|3|geographictointrinsic(reference, lat, lon): [columns, rows] as places of the grid, counted from 1 at the middle of the first sample
intrinsictogeographic||3|3|intrinsictogeographic(reference, column, row): [lat, lon] of a place of the grid
geographictodiscrete||3|3|geographictodiscrete(reference, lat, lon): [rows, columns] of the samples that hold those places; undefined outside the limits
worldtointrinsic||3|3|worldtointrinsic(reference, x, y): [columns, rows] as places of a grid of a plane
intrinsictoworld||3|3|intrinsictoworld(reference, column, row): [x, y] of a place of a grid of a plane
worldtodiscrete||3|3|worldtodiscrete(reference, x, y): [rows, columns] of the samples that hold those places of a plane
geointerp||4|5|geointerp(grid, reference, lat, lon[, "nearest" | "linear" | "cubic"]): the values of a grid at places between its samples (along straight lines unless said; cubic: the convolution of Keys over sixteen samples); undefined outside the limits
mapinterp||4|5|mapinterp(grid, reference, x, y[, method]): the same for a grid of a plane
ltln2val||4|5|ltln2val(grid, reference, lat, lon[, method]): as geointerp, the nearest sample unless a method is said
geocrop||4|4|geocrop(grid, reference, [south, north], [west, east]): [the part of the grid whose cells reach into those limits (whose postings lie in them), its reference]
mapcrop||4|4|mapcrop(grid, reference, [x from, to], [y from, to]): the same for a grid of a plane
georesize||3|4|georesize(grid, reference, scale or [scale of the rows, of the columns][, "nearest" | "linear" | "cubic"]): [the grid with that many times the samples in the same limits, its reference]; cubic unless said
mapresize||3|4|mapresize(grid, reference, scale[, method]): the same for a grid of a plane
gradientm||2|3|gradientm(grid, reference[, ellipsoid]): [the course of the way down in degrees clockwise from north (undefined where the ground is level), the slope in degrees, the rise towards north and towards east for a unit of length]: differences of neighbours over the lengths between them on the ellipsoid (WGS84 in metres unless given)
areamat||2|3|areamat(grid of true and false, reference[, ellipsoid]): [the area of the cells that are true, the area of a cell of every row]: as shares of the whole surface, with an ellipsoid in the square of its unit
los2||6|9|los2(grid of heights, reference, lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2[, height of the eye above the ground[, of the target[, radius of the earth]]]): [whether the second place is seen from the first over the ground on a round earth, whether every place on the way is, the ways from the watcher, the heights of the ground, the latitudes, the longitudes]; metres, the radius 6371000
viewshed||4|7|viewshed(grid of heights, reference, lat, lon[, height of the eye[, of the targets[, radius]]]): the matrix with 1 for every sample that is seen from that place and 0 for the others
arcgridread|readgeoraster|1|1|arcgridread("file.asc"): [grid, reference] of a grid of ESRI in text (ncols, nrows, xllcorner, yllcorner, cellsize, NODATA_value, then the rows from the north); the values that say no data are undefined
arcgridwrite||3|3|arcgridwrite("file.asc", grid, reference): writes a grid of square cells as a grid of ESRI in text
worldfileread||3|3|worldfileread("file.tfw", "geographic" | "planar", [rows, columns]): the reference of a grid from its world file (six numbers: the sizes of a cell and the middle of the first one)
worldfilewrite||2|2|worldfilewrite(reference, "file.tfw"): writes the world file of a reference of cells
egm96geoid||1|2|egm96geoid(lat, lon): the height of the geoid above the ellipsoid of WGS84 in metres, from the Earth Gravitational Model 1996 to degree and order 360 with its correction, summed as the program F477 of the agency that made it; egm96geoid(reference): the matrix of the heights at the samples of a grid
geoidheight||2|3|geoidheight(lat, lon[, "EGM96"]): the same by another name
geoshow||2|*|geoshow(grid, reference): the grid as colours, north at the top and west at the left (as matrixplot)
contourm||2|*|contourm(grid, reference[, heights]): its lines of equal height (as contour)
surfm|meshm|2|*|surfm(grid, reference): the grid as a surface over the longitudes and latitudes (as surf)
